{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_168","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_168","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Operand collection\n\nCollection writes AUIPC's PC into the first EX operand register and writes the OP-IMM, LUI, or AUIPC immediate into the second. Decode derives these roles from validated controls. Collection qualifies the adjacent writer for each role on the same edge as the data. The ALU, multiplier, and register comparisons share the post-bypass operands. Stores retain the forwarded register value for byte-lane formatting.\n\nRVFI captures both encoded register sources from the youngest eligible MEM or WB writer, with independent adjacent-writer matches. EX applies the existing fault-qualified bypass and formatted load value. A divide captures its trace operands at issue.\n\nThe pipeline tests cover immediate fields that alias adjacent destinations and four stalled bus cycles for each store width. They check both RVFI sources, ALU results, accepted bus transactions, and store retirement. A JALR test exercises two different bases that reach the same predicted target after bit zero is cleared, followed by a misaligned target.\n\nThe operand fold alone measured 80.985 MHz across three placements. Timing identified divider feedback and redirect decisions as limits. The selected implementation extends collection to prepare direct control targets in the displacement register and the predicted base for JALR in its spare second operand slot. The JALR and effective-address adders still consume the original displacement.\n\nThe three divider lanes with the most serial steps now resolve pairs of quotient bits with parallel trial subtractions. The shared operand path supplies the divisor at issue, when the divider captures three times its low 18-bit magnitude. This preserves the original seven-round schedule and increases logic area.\n\nVerilator lint passed. The single-channel ALTOPS formal gate passed all 53 checks on its first run against the selected RTL. All 110 cocotb cases passed, including the real divider arithmetic tests. Co-simulation passed on five ELFs. With both buses randomly stalled, CoreMark matched the five canonical CRCs and reported successful validation between markers 12,762 and 3,251,191, a total of 3,238,429 cycles.\n\nGowin placement 1 measured 84.996 MHz, below the 87.277 MHz target. The other two placement runs are pending.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
