{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short; git diff --check; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n# Trailing radix-4 divider trials\n\nEach round consumes dividend bits from the MSB in groups of (1, 2, 2) in the 4-bit lane, (2, 2) in the 11-bit lane, and (1, 2) in the 18-bit lane. The 25-bit and 32-bit lanes use two and one binary restores per round, respectively.\n\nAt issue, the divider registers three times the low 18 bits of the divisor magnitude in 20 bits. Each paired trial subtracts D, 2D, and 3D in parallel, retaining the borrow through a W+3-bit subtraction. The borrow from 2D selects between the two lower and two upper remainder candidates. The divider retains its prefix seeds, seven-round schedule, signed correction, divide-by-zero result, and result-hold behavior. The implementation follows the proposed architecture.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with zero warnings.\n- All 23 ALU tests and both multiplier tests passed.\n- An SMT check of the synthesized RTL proved that issue captures the correct 3D value and that it holds between issues. For each of the three narrow lanes, a round produced the same quotient and remainder as serial binary restores for every nonzero divisor fitting the lane, every incoming remainder below that divisor, and arbitrary quotient-register contents.\n- Co-simulation passed on selftest and three random programs, each with and without memory stalls. The stalled CoreMark run matched all five canonical CRCs and completed ten iterations in 3,044,309 marker cycles.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
