{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_126","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Shared instruction replay RAM\n\nFetch stores control and ordinary instructions in one synchronous 4,096 × 39-bit table. The word PC selects the row. Each row contains the instruction, six upper PC tag bits and a valid bit. EX uses its existing control and successor eligibility checks to train the table.\n\nThe 64-entry BTB keeps its original index, targets and two-bit counters. Its tag, target and counter now occupy one 32-bit metadata word. Fetch checks the opcode of the selected instruction before predicting a transfer. This also covers an ordinary replay word that replaced a control at the same PC.\n\nAn external response has priority. A held fetch rereads its row. EX redirects cancel speculative reads, and a read/write collision suppresses the affected response. Reset and FENCE.I restart a 4,096-cycle validity sweep through the RAM write port. External instructions can execute throughout the sweep.\n\nThe pipeline suite checks cold fetch, warm replay through the seventeenth word, recovery with and without an external response, held loads, replacement instructions, aliases 0x4000 bytes apart and same-row collisions with equal and different tags. A repeated FENCE.I test holds fetch across the end of the sweep and checks the last physical row.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
