{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_131","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; git diff --check; git check-ignore cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_131","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; git diff --check; git check-ignore cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Registered replay recovery\n\nIF uses the registered recovery bit on the cycle that it presents the redirect target. It suppresses the buffered word and the outstanding row, issues a ready external word immediately, and starts a sequential RAM read. A stalled fetch retains the buffer only on a qualified hit, so a discarded word cannot reappear on the next cycle. FENCE.I still clears the group validity mask. A simultaneous fill and read of the same row still suppresses that response.\n\nEX/MEM holds its payload during data backpressure. Reset and divide bubbles clear retirement validity, forwarding permission, and both memory enables. MEM/WB holds the preceding writer during a data wait and clears retirement validity, allowing collection to use the value throughout the wait. Reset clears its writer permission.\n\n## Timing-driven changes\n\nThe replay and bubble changes alone measured 71.917 MHz median. The worst paths still reached the data RAM address and the recovered PC. The following changes preserve the issue and retirement schedule.\n\n- EX/MEM captures the control-transfer target, fall-through PC, and direction separately. IF selects between these registered values during recovery. The same recovery bit annuls the two younger instructions, and the recovered instruction can enter decode on the original edge.\n- EX/MEM has a dedicated data address. The additions for the saved base, adjacent ALU result, and forwarded load run in parallel. JALR shares the base addition. The ALU forwarding permissions clock alongside their producer.\n- For a load-dependent BEQ or BNE, MEM compares the unshifted RAM bytes against the other operand. The byte mask selects the participating lanes, and a separate check verifies the sign or zero extension.\n- After these changes, the divide result register limited placement 1. The seventh round already stores the final quotient and remainder in each lane. MEM now selects those registers directly, before the next divide can overwrite them. The divider retains its seven rounds and its existing transfer handshake.\n\n## Local checks\n\nVerilator lint passed with no warnings. All six cocotb suites passed. Selftest and all three random retirement traces matched the ISS with and without stalls.\n\nCoreMark completed ten iterations with both bus stalls in 3,273,911 bracketed cycles (12,762 through 3,286,673). All five expected CRCs and the validation banner matched, and the harness reported no out-of-bounds access.\ncores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
