{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'ls -l /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh; nproc; cat /proc/meminfo | head; cat formal/checks_si.cfg; cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md | head -90'", "aggregated_output": "-rwxr-xr-x 1 nobody nogroup 24968 Jul 10  2025 /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\n20\nMemTotal:       65423396 kB\nMemFree:        59206504 kB\nMemAvailable:   62130436 kB\nBuffers:          254680 kB\nCached:          3301436 kB\nSwapCached:       109620 kB\nActive:          3018848 kB\nInactive:        2058704 kB\nActive(anon):    1434604 kB\nInactive(anon):   184180 kB\n[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r1s0 (regression, +42.82%): Testing 15 divisor multiples in parallel raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 7.774 MHz, but chaining eight radix-16 remainder selections in one EX cycle kept the core below the multicycle divider's 43.9 MHz.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r1s1 (improvement, +705.78%): Registering the shared EX divider after every six restoring steps shortened the critical path, and CoreMark incurred no divide stalls because it executed no DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU between timing markers.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Predicting branches with 32 two-bit counters and steering JALs in fetch avoided one-cycle EX redirects and cut CoreMark cycles by 6.4%, but the combinational EX divider kept Fmax near 5.5 MHz versus 43.9 MHz with multicycle division.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r2s0 (regression, +8.63%): Testing D, 2D and 3D in parallel cut each six-bit divider round from six dependent remainder selections to three, raising Fmax until a path from the register file set the limit.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r2s1 (regression, +5.00%): Splitting at divisor magnitude 2^14 raised Fmax at the same six-round latency because small divisors need only 15-bit trial subtractors and seeding the wide remainder with the top 14 dividend bits cuts the 33-bit subtractor chain from six to three per round.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r2s2 (improvement, +9.91%): Storing the control-transfer instruction word in the 64-entry BTB let a tag hit satisfy fetch during imem stalls even when the predictor chose fall-through, saving 154,266 CoreMark cycles over the same BTB with replay disabled.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r3s1 (regression, +12.14%): Replaying ordinary instructions from a 256-word backedge region during imem stalls saved 504,231 CoreMark cycles over BTB-only replay, while the round winner could start replay on either branch outcome and gained 14.0% throughput.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r3s2 (improvement, +14.00%): Separate 16-word sequences at each BTB index kept taken and fall-through paths from overwriting each other's PC-tagged instructions for replay during imem stalls, saving 39,471 CoreMark cycles over a shared sequence.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
