{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/test/test_multiplier.py; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Check the combinational RV32M multiplier used in MEM.\"\"\"\nimport random\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.triggers import Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import ALU_MUL, ALU_MULH, ALU_MULHSU, ALU_MULHU\n\nMASK32 = 0xffffffff\n\n\nasync def _check(dut, op, a, b, expected):\n    dut.op.value = op\n    dut.a.value = a\n    dut.b.value = b\n    await Timer(1, \"ns\")\n    actual = int(dut.out.value)\n    assert actual == expected, f\"op={op} {a:#x} * {b:#x}: {actual:#x} != {expected:#x}\"\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def multiply_vectors(dut):\n    for op, a, b, expected in (\n        (ALU_MUL, 3, 4, 12),\n        (ALU_MUL, MASK32, MASK32, 1),\n        (ALU_MULH, 0x80000000, 2, MASK32),\n        (ALU_MULH, 0x80000000, 0x80000000, 0x40000000),\n        (ALU_MULH, 1, 1, 0),\n        (ALU_MULHU, MASK32, 2, 1),\n        (ALU_MULHU, MASK32, MASK32, 0xfffffffe),\n        (ALU_MULHSU, 0x80000000, MASK32, 0x80000000),\n        (ALU_MULHSU, MASK32, MASK32, MASK32),\n    ):\n        await _check(dut, op, a, b, expected)\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def full_width_multiply(dut):\n    edges = (0, 1, 2, 0x7fffffff, 0x80000000, 0x80000001, MASK32)\n    pairs = [(a, b) for a in edges for b in edges]\n    rng = random.Random(0x33)\n    pairs.extend((rng.getrandbits(32), rng.getrandbits(32)) for _ in range(128))\n    for a, b in pairs:\n        sa = a - (1 << 32) if a >> 31 else a\n        sb = b - (1 << 32) if b >> 31 else b\n        await _check(dut, ALU_MUL, a, b, (a * b) & MASK32)\n        await _check(dut, ALU_MULH, a, b, ((sa * sb) >> 32) & MASK32)\n        await _check(dut, ALU_MULHSU, a, b, ((sa * b) >> 32) & MASK32)\n        await _check(dut, ALU_MULHU, a, b, (a * b) >> 32)\n# RV32M completion in MEM\n\nMEM/WB is the first bypass point for all eight RV32M operations. One signed 33-by-33 multiplier uses the forwarded operands in EX/MEM. The divider consumes five dividend bits in each of six EX rounds. MEM uses the held remainder, quotient, and two saved dividend bits to finish the division and restore the sign. MEM/WB can capture that result on the same edge that EX issues the next independent divide.\n\nThe existing load-use interlock now covers M operations. Independent multiplies retire one cycle apart. An adjacent consumer waits one additional cycle. The interlock compares both raw source fields, so an immediate field that matches the preceding destination can also incur a bubble. The target uses the single RVFI channel declared in its core.yaml.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with real arithmetic and with ALTOPS.\n- Local riscv-formal passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the first run.\n- All six cocotb suites passed. The ALU test entry point runs the MEM multiplier vectors against their own top module. Divider vectors cover the boundary at 2^30, all four tail patterns, sign correction, division by zero, reset, and result holds. Pipeline tests check RVFI operands, results, retirement order, dependency timing, stalled loads, wrong-path issue, and consecutive independent divides.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the reference retirement trace with and without bus stalls. The stalled ten-iteration CoreMark run validated all five CRCs and completed in 3,548,404 cycles between markers.\n\n## Local FPGA result\n\nGowin placements 0, 1, and 2 reached 49.085, 49.506, and 49.506 MHz. The median gives **139.52 CoreMark iterations/s**, 14.01% above the supplied 122.37 baseline. Placement 0 used 3,648 LUT4s and two DSPs. Its worst setup path ran from the register file to fetch. The divider feedback path was within 0.005 ns of it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
