{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_81","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'date -u && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"Sat Nov 21 15:38:22 UTC 2026\n# Registered data-bus address and EX load issue\n\nTen CoreMark iterations with both stall generators took **3,288,627 marker cycles**, saving 259,777 cycles. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 49.834, 49.315 and 49.315 MHz. The median gives **149.96 iterations/s**, 7.48% above the 139.52 baseline. Placement 0 used 4,406 logic cells and 1,109 registers.\n\n## Implementation\n\nA single clock-enabled register drives the byte address. A store or unfinished load reserves its MEM slot. Otherwise decode can register a load address and grant together. EX checks instruction validity, load width, alignment and the ordinary RAM range before asserting a read. Acceptance captures the raw word for MEM/WB and RVFI and a formatted result for EX/MEM forwarding. A denied read continues at the same address in MEM and retains the dependent instruction's interlock.\n\nAddress generation gives the youngest writer priority. An EX ALU result supports zero displacement. An accepted EX word load, an available MEM result, or the write-through register file supports a signed displacement. Decode squashes the valid bit and controls independently of the operand payload.\n\n## Timing decisions\n\nGowin initially reported a critical path from the fetch tag comparison through the register file and load-address adder. A dedicated register-file read port now takes candidate load fields directly from the external instruction or successor RAM. This relies on the BTB containing only control instructions. The validated ID/EX instruction still qualifies every EX read.\n\nSuccessor replay checks validity against the registered read path. Registered write information preserves first-allocation and same-word collision behavior while shortening the EX redirect path. The final placement reports identify divider feedback as the worst setup path.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. All six cocotb suites passed, including 35 pipeline tests. The bus monitor checks held requests and records every accepted transfer. Directed tests check consecutive pointer loads and branches, raw and extended subwords, fallback bubbles, stalled stores, writer priority, MMIO, flushes and traps.\n- Co-simulation matched selftest and all three random retirement traces with and without bus stalls. CoreMark reported all five canonical CRCs, the validation message, and zero out-of-bounds accesses. The start and stop markers occurred at cycles 12,248 and 3,300,875.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
