{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_70","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; git status --short'","aggregated_output":"# Linked instruction replay\n\nTen CoreMark iterations took **3,223,900 marker cycles** with both bus stalls, saving 175,450 cycles against the 3,399,350-cycle baseline. Median Gowin Fmax was **60.741 MHz**, yielding **188.41 iterations/s**, 10.56% above 170.41 iterations/s.\n\n## Implementation\n\nThe 2,048 × 60 synchronous RAM holds the instruction, PC tag, control flag, two-bit counter and predicted successor. EX fills it through a registered update. Each read captures the index, initialized mask and group validity with the row. The fetch PC must match the full reconstructed address before issue. A simultaneous fill of the requested row makes that response a miss.\n\nThe read address follows the raw successor in normal operation. Registered sequential and retry modes recover after redirects, misses and backpressure. On the first held hit, a one-entry buffer saves the verified row while the RAM completes its outstanding read. The response then stays frozen through buffer release. A ready instruction-bus word takes priority, including on release. FENCE.I clears group validity, cancels the pending fill and redirects to refetch its successor.\n\nID/EX captures the available payload during a flush and clears its valid bit. Validity qualifies late-result hazards, register writes and both memory enables. The dependency and load-bypass checks use the selected fetch word.\n\n## Timing decision\n\nThe first implementation reached 57.407 MHz median with the EX redirect driving the fetch PC enable. The delivered version registers the full EX recovery target alongside the normal next PC and uses a registered redirect bit to select between them. This preserves the recovery cycle and removes the wide PC enable from the late target-comparison path.\n\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 57.123 MHz |\n| 1 | 60.741 MHz |\n| 2 | 60.741 MHz |\n\nOption 0 used 3,881 logic cells, 1,190 registers, 15 BSRAMs and two DSPs. Placement remains material. Option 0 falls below the 57.466 MHz acceptance threshold, while the scored median exceeds it.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with all warnings enabled by the requested command.\n- All 53 ALTOPS formal checks passed on the final design on its first run.\n- All 101 cocotb cases passed. Directed checks cover replay of the seventeenth word, load-dependent issue across four data stalls, both directions of an 8 KiB index alias, a cold slot in a live group, same-row fill collisions, JALR recovery with a buffered store, FENCE.I invalidation and an external replacement on buffer release.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the reference retirement traces with and without bus stalls.\n- CoreMark reached EBREAK with the validation message, all five canonical CRCs and no out-of-bounds access. The start and stop markers occurred at cycles 12,291 and 3,236,191.\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
