{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_123","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Adjacent forwarding\n\nOperand collection now registers separate ALU/link and aligned-load enables for each source. EX qualifies the producer's write, using its effective byte offset for load alignment and suppressing the link on a control-target fault. The consumer keeps the older value selected by collection when the adjacent instruction traps.\n\nFor an adjacent BEQ or BNE, collection captures the opposite operand along with the load width, signedness, and byte mask. EX compares the four raw memory lanes in parallel and checks the expected operand's upper bits. The sideband registers share the operand register's stall enable. RVFI still records the formatted load value, and a dependent load can use the raw LW response as its address base.\n\nEX/MEM clears validity and side-effect controls on reset or a divide bubble. Fetch selects external or replay data by bus readiness, while the ready-or-hit signal qualifies issue.\n\n## Timing-driven changes\n\nThe initial implementation reached a 71.922 MHz median. Its timing reports showed paths through both the general ALU and the divider. The final RTL registers the effective address separately in EX/MEM. The low bits of that same sum qualify the adjacent load. MEM uses the registered address for the bus, alignment, masks, and load formatting.\n\nThe real divider's seven rounds already clock the completed quotient and remainder into each lane on the EX/MEM transfer edge. MEM now selects from those held registers. This removes the lane-selection logic from the last subtraction's setup path and saves the duplicate result registers. MEM/WB samples the result by the edge that can start the next divide.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with all requested warnings enabled.\n- Single-retire ALTOPS formal passed all 53 checks on the first run.\n- All six cocotb suites passed. The pipeline tests cover each byte lane and halfword, single-byte differences in active lanes, extension-only mismatches, same-register comparisons, stalled responses, and faulting loads and jumps over older writers.\n- Selftest and three random programs matched the reference ISS with zero waits and with both buses stalled.\n- Ten CoreMark iterations took 3,273,911 cycles between the MMIO markers. UART reported the five expected CRCs and `Correct operation validated.`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
