{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_112","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short; git diff --stat; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv       | 160 +++++++++++++++++--------------------------\n cores/bench/test/test_alu.py |  53 +++++++++++++-\n 2 files changed, 114 insertions(+), 99 deletions(-)\n# Reciprocal-table divider\n\nThe five restoring lanes in `rtl/alu.sv` are replaced by one synchronous 4,096-word reciprocal ROM. On issue the divider captures unsigned magnitudes, sign information, and a flag for a zero-divisor quotient. The ROM read and scaling take two rounds. Rounds 3 through 6 reuse one high-product multiplier and one low-word multiply-add for both estimates. Round 7 uses the borrow from the residual subtraction to select the exact result and registers the requested magnitude for MEM.\n\nROM row zero contains zero, and row one contains the saturated reciprocal `0xffffffff`. The quotient accumulator holds the first estimate and then the sum. Registering only the selected magnitude removes a 32-bit result register and moves the quotient/remainder selection ahead of MEM. For a zero divisor, the residual already holds the dividend magnitude throughout, so ordinary sign restoration gives the required remainder. The ordinary multiplier retains its EX/MEM bypass.\n\n## Arithmetic and pipeline checks\n\n- Verilator full-core lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- Local formal passed on the first run, with all 53 single-retirement ALTOPS checks passing. The integer bound and RTL comparisons below exercise the real arithmetic.\n- All seven pytest checks passed, including the cocotb ALU, multiplier, and pipeline suites. The new integer bound check covers all 43,007 nonempty buckets below `2^31`. The largest bound was 0.667237 at divisor `0x55500000`.\n- Directed RTL tests cover bucket edges at every scale, maximum dividends, exact multiples and their neighbors. For `0xffffffff / 2049` the result held across a downstream stall was 2,096,128, followed by a back-to-back remainder of 1,023.\n- A separate Verilator sweep compared 3,818,000 seven-round DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU operations against integer arithmetic, including the endpoints of every normalized bucket and randomized operands. All matched.\n- Cosimulation passed for five ELFs. Selftest and three random programs matched the ISS with and without bus stalls. With both stalls enabled, CoreMark measured 3,044,309 cycles between markers, emitted all five expected CRCs, and printed the validation banner.\n\n## FPGA timing\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 87.454, 90.588, and 90.588 MHz. At the median clock, ten CoreMark iterations in 3,044,309 cycles give **297.57 iterations/s**, 6.57% above the supplied 279.21 baseline.\n\nOption 0 used 4,484 logic cells, 1,848 registers, 33 BSRAMs, and 6 DSPs. The final residual-to-result path still limited this placement. In both median placements the worst path ran from the registered rs1 bypass select to the EX/MEM result. A trial that precomputed the incremented quotient in round six reached 83.730 MHz median, so the delivered RTL keeps the increment in round seven.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
