et RAM feedback through `u_hazard` was only 0.007 ns shorter than the EX divider path in the median placement.\n17:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r7s0 (regression, +6.05%): Sharing one 4,096-row PC-indexed replay RAM between control and ordinary instructions removed a 32-bit fetch mux input, raising prototype Fmax from 56.012 to 58.147 MHz on Gowin placement 1 while saving 10,492 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations.\n22:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r8s2 (regression, +2.96%): Using displacement bit 5 to predict the carry into address bit 6 let verified loads bypass RAM waits and saved 145,087 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations in the prototype, but registering the corrected low address in EX/MEM still yielded only 193.99 iterations/s against the 197.08 target.\n23:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r9s0 (regression, +6.62%): Masking wrong-path replay hits with `recovering` took the load-dependent redirect off the replay resets but left the ALU-to-RAM address path critical at 71.917 MHz median, while parallel address sums, byte-lane load comparisons and reuse of the divider’s final lane registers raised the median to 76.247 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n31:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r11s2 (improvement, +8.37%): Forwarding a resident-page word predicted during operand collection through EX/MEM removed the adjacent load-use bubble on a verified hit, while a second probe at the forwarded EX address could recover from a stale base guess without a bus read.\n35:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r13s0 (regression, +0.99%): Stalling a dependent consumer for one cycle when a load or link faults let registered RAW matches select the adjacent bypass without waiting for EX alignment checks, but the baseline ALU and redirect paths already had nearly the same delay as the bypass path.\n36:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r13s1 (regression, +1.52%): Capturing fallback operands independently of the adjacent writer's alignment check moved the critical path to the EX/MEM bypass through rs1 and the integer ALU back to EX/MEM, limiting median Fmax to 93.627 MHz against a 96.48 MHz target.\n38:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261121-001-r14s0 (regression, -0.64%): Predicting the carry or borrow into bit 16 from `B[15]` and repairing only adjacent dependents added 2,475 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, while the path from load predictor RAM through MEM to `id_ex_w.alu_a_base` limited median Fmax to 96.718 MHz versus the baseline’s 97.260 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
