{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Four-bit fetch steering\n\nFetch compares PC bits [5:2] with the registered replay index to select a next-PC hint. A buffered word has already passed the full PC check. Each synchronous read samples the addressed initialized bit alongside the row and index, and all three hold while buffered. The existing live bit suppresses collisions and invalidated reads.\n\nAt fill, the stored counter MSB is gated by the control bit, so ordinary instructions carry a zero taken hint. A ready external word must decode as a branch, JAL, or JALR to use the hint. Replay issue and prediction-hit reporting still require exact PC verification. The prediction bundle carries the direction and target used by fetch through to EX, including on a partial match.\n\nThe implementation follows the proposed RTL plan. The directed test runs the same JALR alias at P + 0x2000 twice, once with an immediately ready response and once after six wait cycles. It checks the held PC, the fetch of the stale target on release, EX recovery, the retired PCs and instruction words, and a single correct-path store containing the JALR link.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint completed without warnings.\n- All 47 pipeline tests and all five other unit-test runners passed.\n- Local formal passed all 53 checks on the first run with the configured single-retire wrapper.\n- Cosimulation passed for selftest and three random programs with and without stalls. Ten CoreMark iterations with both stall generators passed all five canonical CRC checks and printed the validation banner.\n\n## Timing and fitness\n\nCoreMark used 3,044,442 marker cycles, 133 more than the champion. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 86.623, 90.760, and 90.760 MHz respectively. The median gives 298.12 iterations per second, 6.69% below the champion. Promotion at this cycle count requires a median above 101.738 MHz.\n\nFor options 1 and 2, the worst reported path starts at the registered EX result and ends at the fetch read-mode register. Option 0 is limited by a path from the registered EX operand to the EX result.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
