alls saved 504,231 CoreMark cycles over BTB-only replay, while the round winner could start replay on either branch outcome and gained 14.0% throughput.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r3s2 (improvement, +14.00%): Separate 16-word sequences at each BTB index \n…[4549 of 7621 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e23a6ea7f5f38aa9]…\n Masking wrong-path replay hits with `recovering` took the load-dependent redirect off the replay resets but left the ALU-to-RAM address path critical at 71.917 MHz median, while parallel address sums, byte-lane load comparisons and reuse of the divider’s final lane registers raised the median to 76.247 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r9s1 (improvement, +9.54%): Registering per-source bypass enables during producer EX removed `EX/MEM.mem_read` from the next address calculation, and comparing active RAM bytes in parallel with an extension check on the other operand let dependent BEQ/BNE resolve without waiting for load formatting.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r9s2 (regression, +5.88%): Looking up load words during operand collection let adjacent branches and pointer loads use a registered operand in EX while MEM verified the hit, but the prototype’s 4.1% reduction in CoreMark cycles could not offset its lower median Fmax relative to the round winner (72.784 vs 78.330 MHz).\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
