{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261121-001-r4s0.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r4s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "Wrote and schema-validated the `micro_opt` hypothesis. It shortens the divider’s critical path by using six narrow trials or three full-width trials per clock, with both modes sharing the existing registers. [Hypothesis YAML](</srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-astra_xhigh-v2-rep4/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261121-001-r4s0-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r4s0.yaml>)\n\nThe scratch prototype reached **132.89 CoreMark iterations/s**, **8.59% above** the champion. Median Fmax was 46.666 MHz, and ten iterations took 3,511,720 marker cycles. All five CRCs matched, and all 52 ALU and pipeline tests passed.id: hyp-20261121-001-r4s0\ntitle: Pack two divisor precisions into the existing six-round divider registers\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  Gowin traced the champion's 170-cell worst path from remainder_q[1] to\n  remainder_q[13] through six full-width restoring steps. This limits every\n  instruction to 42.974 MHz even though CoreMark executes zero divides or\n  remainders between its timing markers. A previous magnitude split reduced\n  the arithmetic depth using separate state for each precision. Both modes\n  can use the champion's existing remainder and quotient registers if each\n  round always shifts the quotient by six positions.\nhypothesis: |\n  Capture one mode bit from the unsigned divisor magnitude D. For\n  0 < D < 2^14, every restored remainder fits in 14 bits, so all six\n  trials per clock need only 15 bits each. For D >= 2^14, the top 14 bits\n  of the dividend magnitude A are already smaller than D. Seed the remainder\n  with that prefix and resolve the remaining 18 bits in three full-width\n  trials per clock. Pack those 18 bits into six groups in the same 36-bit\n  shift register. One pair of state registers then serves both precisions\n  with the original six-round latency.\n\n  A scratch prototype on the current core reached 45.815, 46.666 and\n  46.666 MHz on Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2. Option zero used\n  3,640 LUT4s and 827 registers, up 85 LUT4s and one register. Its worst\n  path\n…[1090 of 4162 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fd2b0305c65ad247]…\nt Q\n      left by six and insert either six narrow quotient bits or three zeros\n      followed by the three wide bits. After six rounds the narrow quotient\n      is Q[31:0]. Gather the low three bits of each six-bit group in order\n      for the wide quotient. Fill its upper 14 bits with copies of D==0.\n      For D=0 the wide residual reconstructs A and every quotient bit is one.\n      Apply the existing result selection and sign correction. Retain reset,\n      done/accept behavior, INT_MIN/-1 semantics and all ALTOPS equations.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU against the integer oracle on both\n      sides of D=0x4000, including negative signed operands and transitions\n      through D=0. At D=0x4000 use dividends q*D+r with a different octal\n      digit in each of the six quotient groups. Exercise each group alone\n      and r in {0,1,D-1} to catch reversed groups, dropped bits and borrow\n      errors. Retain the exact six-round deadline and held-result checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
