alls saved 504,231 CoreMark cycles over BTB-only replay, while the round winner could start replay on either branch outcome and gained 14.0% throughput.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r3s2 (improvement, +14.00%): Separate 16-word sequences at each BTB index \n…[6635 of 9707 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1d6190c794da3df6]…\nr feedback, the path from `bypass_rs1_q` through the multiplier DSP to the registered low product limited median Fmax to 84.996 MHz.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r11s1 (regression, +4.21%): Replacing the five restoring lanes with a reciprocal lookup and two uses of the EX multiplier made the path from data RAM to the fetch recovery PC critical at 86.950 MHz median, with CoreMark still taking 3,238,429 cycles.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r11s2 (improvement, +8.37%): Forwarding a resident-page word predicted during operand collection through EX/MEM removed the adjacent load-use bubble on a verified hit, while a second probe at the forwarded EX address could recover from a stale base guess without a bus read.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r12s0 (improvement, +8.51%): Pairing trials in the 18-bit divider lane moved the critical path to the 4-bit lane's five serial restores, and resolving trailing quotient-bit pairs with radix-4 trials across all three narrow lanes raised Fmax at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
