{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git show -s --format=%B 21fd115 && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r3s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r3s2: Replay the instruction sequence on each predicted branch path (+14.0%)\n\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r3s2\ntitle: \"Replay the instruction sequence on each predicted branch path\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion took 4,043,251 marker cycles. Replaying the branch instruction\n  on a branch target buffer (BTB) hit saved 154,266 cycles in the previous experiment.\n  The BTB predicts where execution continues, but fetch still waits for imem\n  on the ordinary instructions along that path. Repeated loop backedges and\n  calls give the predictor several consecutive fetches it can anticipate.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give each BTB index two successor sequences, one per branch outcome. At a\n  BTB hit, the predicted direction selects the sequence. Read its first word\n  on the same clock edge that installs the predicted next PC. As fetch advances,\n  predict successive words along that path, up to 16 ordinary instructions.\n  An EX correction selects the sequence for the actual outcome. Each saved\n  word carries its own PC tag. Checking it against the current fetch PC rejects\n  a stale sequence when a JALR changes target or two branches share an index.\n  The ready external response has first priority, then the BTB branch word,\n  then the predicted successor word.\n\n  A scratch RTL prototype completed ten canonical CoreMark iterations in\n  3,511,720 marker cycles with all five CRCs, 13.15% fewer cycles than the\n  champion. Sharing one 16-word sequence between the two directions cost\n  39,471 extra cycles. Separate sequences reached 41.280, 42.159 and\n  42.159 MHz across the three Gowin placement options. The median gives\n  120.05 iterations/s, 11.84% above 107.34. At this cycle count the\n  implementation must exceed 39.429 MHz to clear the acceptance margin.\n  Placement option zero used 3,529 LUT4s and 14 BSRAMs. Its worst path ran\n  from the register file through EX to the successor read-valid register.\n  The prototype passed 53 single-retirement formal checks and 92 cocotb\n  cases, including four directed rep\n…[1591 of 4663 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8ce94b1069cd5139]…\nIF connection.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export advancing and the resolved aligned transfer direction. Use the\n      existing BTB write qualification to identify an anchor. Allow a successor\n      payload write for a decoded legal non-branch, non-jump instruction at an\n      aligned RAM PC on EX advance. FENCE.I invalidation overrides the write.\n      Keep recovery and RVFI next PC driven by the full architectural resolution.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a loop and withhold imem throughout its body, including across a\n      load-use interlock and a four-cycle dmem hold. Check that a 17-word body\n      can replay the first 16 and must wait for word 17. Change branch direction,\n      alias two source branches to one index and retarget a JALR with a store\n      on the old path. Check the RVFI PC pairs and store strobes. Execute\n      FENCE.I, revisit the same branch and require its first successor to wait\n      for the replacement instruction on the bus.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
