{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r9s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-002-r9s1\ntitle: Qualify adjacent forwarding while the producer executes\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's median placement has two setup paths within 0.026 ns.\n  One runs from EX/MEM.mem_read through the next instruction's ALU to\n  data RAM. The other runs from data RAM through load formatting and\n  the dependent branch to fetch recovery. Both consumers already occupy\n  operand collection while their producer is in EX. That is the edge\n  on which to prepare their bypass.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move bypass qualification to the producer's EX cycle. For each source\n  of the waiting consumer, register two enables with its collected\n  operands. One selects the adjacent ALU or link result. The other\n  selects an aligned adjacent load. Use the producer's actual write\n  permission, including control-target faults, and its effective address\n  low bits for load alignment. A faulting producer leaves the older\n  value from operand collection available.\n\n  For a load feeding BEQ or BNE, capture the other collected operand,\n  the producer's width and signedness, and its four-bit byte mask on\n  that same edge. In the consumer's EX cycle, compare the returned\n  word in four byte lanes in parallel. Replicate the expected low byte\n  or halfword across lanes and separately check that its extension\n  equals the upper bits of the expected operand. Reduce equality over\n  the active lanes. This removes load formatting from the branch\n  decision while retaining formatted data for RVFI and the raw LW\n  bypass for a dependent address.\n\n  A scratch prototype matched all five CoreMark CRCs and the champion's\n  3,273,911 marker cycles for ten iterations. Gowin placements\n  0, 1 and 2 reached 71.808, 75.725 and 75.725 MHz. That median yields\n  231.30 iterations/s, 5.90% above 218.42 and above the 228.47\n  acceptance threshold. The added logic cost 35 LUT4s and 85 flip-flops\n  on placement 0. Divider feedback sets the median clock. All\n  53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks, the six cocotb suites and the\n  selftest\n…[1632 of 4704 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f36f7e7cb7ed4f5b]…\nust all equal its bit 7. For LBU they must\n      all be zero. Both source matches name the same register. Use the\n      collected operands on a fault. On EX/MEM\n      advance, capture the payload and separately clear valid,\n      reg_write, mem_read and mem_write on reset or a divide bubble.\n      Hold on backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Apply the same validity discipline to fetch. Select external\n      data when ready and the replay payload otherwise. Gate issue\n      with the existing ready or verified-hit bit so the tag and valid\n      mask can settle in parallel with instruction decode.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise each byte lane, both halfwords, signed and unsigned\n      extension, and equal low lanes with unequal upper bits. Test\n      both source positions, two matches, a misaligned youngest writer\n      over an older value, and changing bus data during a wait. Check\n      branch direction, full RVFI operands and adjacent retirement.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
