{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261121-003-r14s2.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r14s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-003-r14s2\ntitle: Predict a counted loop's exit when writing its next replay successor\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Between the timing markers the champion resolves 529,588 conditional\n  branches and redirects on 43,919 of them. Two branches each produce\n  3,241 redirects in 29,160 visits. Each repeats eight taken outcomes\n  followed by one exit. A two-bit counter quickly learns the backedge\n  but misses nearly every exit. The replay RAM already has a place to\n  store the predicted successor for a branch's next visit.\nhypothesis: |\n  Learn how many consecutive continuation outcomes precede an exit.\n  Use taken as the continuation for a negative displacement and\n  fall-through for a positive displacement. For each of 256 PC-indexed\n  histories store a six-bit saturating run count, the last completed run\n  length and one confidence bit. An exit establishes confidence when\n  two consecutive run lengths match in the range 2 through 62. A changed\n  length, an overrun or saturation clears confidence. The existing\n  two-bit prediction supplies the direction while confidence is clear.\n\n  Read this small table in decode. During operand collection prepare\n  the next history and complete two-bit counter for both possible\n  outcomes, including ordinary bimodal increments and decrements. When a\n  legal, cacheable conditional branch advances in EX, its actual\n  direction selects exactly one training transaction. After the final\n  expected continuation, write fall-through for a backward branch or\n  the target for a forward branch into the normal replay row. After\n  the observed exit, write the continuation for the next invocation.\n  Encode an override as a weak counter in the chosen direction. On\n  every other fill use the existing counter. Fetch can follow the\n  stored link at the existing latency.\n\n  A prototype with a minimum run length of one completed ten iterations in 3,011,033 marker\n  cycles with both stall generators, all five canonical CRCs and the\n  validation banner. That saves 33,276 cycles o\n…[1346 of 4418 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:19bdb3d08ceb54e3]…\nep their current widths.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export jump-or-branch-taken with the existing advancing,\n      legal, aligned, in-RAM update. Use the fully forwarded operands\n      and the existing mismatch, trap and redirect decisions.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Register actual outcome and both counter candidates with the EX\n      fill. Select the counter and use its MSB to prepare both the row counter\n      and its successor so they always describe the same prediction.\n      Ordinary rows, jumps, replay qualification and FENCE.I use the\n      existing fill and lookup rules.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Train a repeated eight-continuation loop, then change its trip\n      count. Check that the warm final continuation predicts the exit,\n      that a wrong count recovers to the architectural PC, and that\n      each store commits once. Cover the forward-exit form, a saturated\n      count and a held branch with an older stalled load.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
