{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r15s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-002-r15s1\ntitle: Split shift execution across EX and MEM with a direct byte-shift path\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  In the champion's 97.260 MHz placement, the path from the EX operand-B\n  select to integer result bit 9 arrives at 15.578 ns, within 0.002 ns\n  of the worst path. The five distance bits currently control a full\n  barrel shift before its result joins the shared EX/MEM bypass word.\n  Moving the byte-distance portion across that register can shorten EX.\n  CoreMark frequently needs an immediately dependent shift result, so\n  the choice of shifts that complete in one cycle determines the cost.\nhypothesis: |\n  Execute SLL, SRL and SRA in two parts. EX performs the shift by bits\n  2 through 0 of the resolved distance. MEM can shift that registered\n  partial result by 0, 8, 16 or 24 more bits. Both stages use the same\n  direction and arithmetic-right sign fill. For immediate distances\n  8, 16 and 24, decode the complete shift in collection and wire the\n  displaced bytes directly into the EX result. Distances below eight\n  already finish in EX. All five RV32 distance bits remain significant\n  for register shifts regardless of how rs2 was forwarded.\n\n  Predecode the direction and byte distance together into enables for\n  the fixed byte routes. Suppress the fine shifter's result select\n  during collection for those immediate forms. This keeps a late\n  direction or byte-route mux off the common integer result. Give the\n  fine shifter its own three-bit distance input so the other ALU\n  functions still receive the original full-width operands.\n\n  Carry a needs-completion bit and coarse shift controls beside the\n  EX/MEM partial word. MEM supplies the exact result to WB and older\n  writer forwarding. An independent successor advances immediately.\n  If an adjacent consumer selected an unfinished shift, use the existing\n  load-repair hold to replace only its matching source or sources before\n  it can execute, redirect, start division or access memory. Hold all\n  shift metadata with its producer on backpressure. Keep load verification\n  and this completion condition distinct.\n\n  A scratch prototype that sent every distance of eight or more through\n  MEM took 3,082,664 marker cycles. Adding the three direct immediate\n  distances reduced that to 3,044,495, only 186 cycles above the champion.\n  Both runs used both stall generators and produced all five canonical\n  CRCs and the validation banner. The fully predecoded byte routes also\n  matched the 3,044,495-cycle result. The fine-only EX prototype without\n  the three byte routes reached 91.488 MHz on placement 1. At the final\n  cycle count promotion requires\n  a median above 101.74 MHz. The timing hypothesis is that collection\n  can absorb the full route decode and shorten the EX result path enough\n  to reach that clock.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: Isolate the shift-distance input from operand B. Support a three-bit fine shift in the pipeline while retaining full-width standalone ALU behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Register byte-route enables from the decoded immediate shift, qualify the fine result selects, and extend the existing adjacent-source repair logic to unfinished shift producers.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Combine the selected fixed byte wiring or fine shift with the ordinary result. Register the coarse count, direction, sign-fill control and completion flag on the EX/MEM transfer.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Finish deferred shifts on the registered partial word and deliver the exact value on both the WB and MEM forwarding paths.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check all 32 distances for both immediate and forwarded register shifts, negative SRA operands, dual-source dependencies, repair under backpressure and an independent successor.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
