only replay, while the round winner could start replay on either branch outcome and gained 14.0% throughput.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r3s2 (improvement, +14.00%): Separate 16-word sequences at each BTB index \n…[9718 of 12790 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:53cd8e8588df3cb1]…\nnd register put the multiplier DSP and per-port result mux on the same EX feedback path, cutting median Fmax from 97.260 to 77.284 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261121-003-r14s2 (regression, -4.36%): Writing the loop exit as the next replay successor after the last expected backedge saved 34,870 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations (1.15%), too little to offset the drop in median Fmax from 97.26 to 92.0 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r15s0 (regression, +0.60%): Finishing ordered comparisons in MEM delayed recovery from a mispredicted ordered branch by one stage, costing the prototype 19,605 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations even though none of the 14,667 set-less-than results had an immediate consumer.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r15s1 (regression, -4.41%): Completing immediate shifts by 8, 16 and 24 bits in EX let adjacent consumers skip the MEM repair stall, leaving the split-shifter prototype just 186 CoreMark cycles above the champion and making lower Fmax the main cost.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
