{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(s)\n if r.get('category')=='micro_opt' or 'multiply' in str(r.get('title')).lower():\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r.get('outcome'),r.get('error',''),str(r.get('motivation',''))[:500])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-e14b75d Baseline retest for cores/bench improvement None \nhyp-20261121-001-r1s0 Resolve four quotient bits per step in the combinational divider regression None The baseline ran at a median 5.442 MHz. Gowin reported a 199.837 ns path\nthrough forwarding and the EX ALU. The single-cycle 32-bit division and\nremainder operators are likely sources of that depth. They constrain the\nclock even on cycles when CoreMark executes an add or a load.\n\nhyp-20261121-001-r2s0 Halve the divider's serial decisions with radix-4 restoring steps regression None Gowin traced the champion's 197-cell worst path from quotient_q[35] to\nremainder_q[12]. Each divider round puts six dependent trial subtractions\nand remainder selections between those registers. CoreMark executed zero\ndivides or remainders between the markers, yet this path sets the clock\nfor all 4,491,485 measured cycles. At that cycle count, 45.89 MHz would\nclear the acceptance margin over the champion's 43.865 MHz.\n\nhyp-20261121-001-r3s0 Shorten the champion's divider feedback path with radix-4 digits regression None On the current BTB-equipped core, Gowin traced a 28.668 ns path from\nremainder_q to quotient_q through six dependent restoring steps. That\npath limits the clock on all 4,043,251 CoreMark marker cycles. The\nearlier radix-4 experiment reached 47.652 MHz on the pre-BTB core.\nAt the champion's cycle count, a median above 45.40 MHz would clear\nthe 4.6% acceptance margin.\n\nhyp-20261121-001-r4s0 Pack two divisor precisions into the existing six-round divider registers regression None Gowin traced the champion's 170-cell worst path from remainder_q[1] to\nremainder_q[13] through six full-width restoring steps. This limits every\ninstruction to 42.974 MHz even though CoreMark executes zero divides or\nremainders between its timing markers. A previous magnitude split reduced\nthe arithmetic depth using separate state for each precision. Both modes\ncan use the champion's existing remainder and quotient registers if each\nround always shifts the quotient by six po\n…[1437 of 4509 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:62aceff5b7d5e7df]…\nslot.\n\nhyp-20261121-001-r8s0 Resolve register dependencies in decode and use one EX operand mux regression None The champion selects WB write-through data at the register file, then\ncompares tags and selects a forwarding source again in EX. Gowin's\nplacement 0 worst path starts at EX/MEM.rd and reaches data RAM through\nforwarding and the ALU. On placement 1, a register-file-to-RAM path\nis within 0.001 ns of the limiting divider path. Both operand paths\ncross the same forwarding network.\n\nhyp-20261121-001-r9s0 Retire wrong-path replay state with the registered recovery bit regression None In the champion's 71.510 MHz placement, the path from data RAM through a\ndependent branch to the fetch read-mode reset arrives at 19.253 ns. It is\nonly 0.026 ns behind the worst address path. A separate effective-address\nregister with parallel base additions reached 72.050 MHz on that placement.\nThe recovery signal still has to cancel several replay states on the edge\nwhere the loaded branch operand first becomes available.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
