{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(s)\n if r['id'].endswith('r4s1') or r['id'].endswith('r5s0') or r['id'].endswith('r8s1'):\n  print(r['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{'motivation': \"A divide that CoreMark never executes between markers sets the champion's\\nclock. Gowin traced its 28.440 ns setup path through 170 cells between\\nremainder registers. Moving two quotient bits out of the divider feedback\\nloop alone reached 44.821 MHz, below the 44.95 MHz acceptance threshold\\nat the champion's cycle count. Timing then ran through operand forwarding\\nand the multiply DSP to the data RAM. The EX/MEM boundary gives both\\noperations a place to finish their arithmetic after operand forwarding.\\n\", 'hypothesis': 'Make MEM/WB the first bypass point for every M-extension result.\\nMultiply the operands already captured in EX/MEM during MEM. For division,\\nuse the existing six clocks in EX to consume the top 30 dividend bits,\\nfive per clock. Carry the instruction across EX/MEM and use its MEM clock\\nfor the last two quotient bits and sign correction. An independent\\ninstruction can issue immediately after a multiply. An adjacent consumer\\nof any M result uses the existing load-use bubble and MEM/WB bypass.\\nThis schedules the work around one common result boundary and keeps the\\nmultiply DSP and final divider selection off the EX address path.\\n\\nA scratch prototype ran ten CoreMark iterations in 3,548,404 bracketed\\ncycles with both bus stalls and all five CRCs validated. The extra\\n36,684 cycles are a 1.04% cost. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached\\n44.792, 46.676 and 46.676 MHz. The median gives 131.54 iterations/s,\\n7.49% above 122.37. Acceptance at this cycle count requires 45.42 MHz.\\nOption 0 used 3,625 LUT4s and two DSPs. Its worst path moved to the\\nregister-file-to-fetch path. Selftest and the three random programs\\nmatched the reference retirement trace with both buses stalled.\\n', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 7, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'On divide issue capture absolute operands and retain the two low\\ndividend bits. Initialize a 30-bit dividend/quotient shift register\\nfrom the upper bits. Run five 30-bit restoring\n…[12352 of 15424 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:70c9c2376bdb5d8b]…\nk exact operands, masks, traps, PC and order.\\nRephase cycle-sensitive stimulus for the extra speculative fetch\\nand adjust the expected recovery gap from two to three cycles in\\nthe consecutive-divides test. In the body-fill collision test,\\nplace two NOPs between the decrement at PC 4 and BNE at PC 16,\\nand target PC 4. Check the collision retry and all three decrement\\nresults. In buffered-word override tests, arrange the older load\\nwait with the word under test still held at fetch. The tested\\nJAL override uses two NOPs between LW and JAL. For FENCE.I,\\nschedule the replacement after the first visit to PC 20 so an\\nearlier speculative fetch of PC 8 cannot consume it. Check both\\nwrong-path slots and the external word selected on release.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Exercise seventh-edge transfer and held completion. Check exact\\nquotient and remainder around each lane boundary, signed overflow\\nand zero divisors, including a subsequent issue with different\\nsigns or operands.\\n'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
