p/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/onehot/id_stage.sv /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/onehot/ex_stage.sv /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/onehot/mem_stage.sv /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/onehot/wb_stage.sv /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/onehot/hazard_unit.sv /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/onehot/forward_unit.sv /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/onehot/core.sv in directory /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/unit/sim_build/core\nINFO     Verilator:runner.py:632 Running command make -j 4 -C /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/unit/sim_build/core -f Vtop.mk VM_TRACE=0 in directory /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/unit/sim_build/core\nINFO     Verilator:runner.py:632 Running command /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/unit/sim_build/core/core in directory /srv/hwebench/tmp/3df75752/883702a3/r10-mul-n38WWL/unit/test\nERROR    Verilator:runner.py:572 ERROR: Failed 1 of 38 tests.\n=========================== short test summary info ============================\nFAILED test/test_pipeline.py::test_pipeline_runner - SystemExit: 1\n1 failed, 5 passed in 25.34s\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_71", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r10s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-001-r10s0\ntitle: Forward the low multiply product from EX/MEM\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  MUL pays the same adjacent-consumer bubble as MULH even though its low\n  32 bits fit the existing EX/MEM forwarding register. Gowin also traces\n  the champion's placement 0 setup limit from EX/MEM.alu_op[2] through\n  the MEM multiplier to a collected operand. In the median placement a\n  similar multiply path arrives only 0.007 ns before the limiting path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move the existing DSP multiply to EX. Give the low product a dedicated\n  enable in the ordinary EX result mux so the next instruction can use\n  it through the existing bypass. Capture the high product separately at\n  EX/MEM and select it in MEM for MULH, MULHU and MULHSU. This removes the\n  live DSP output from operand collection and saves the dependency bubble\n  on MUL. Decode the result enables in collection so adding the low product\n  costs one input to the EX result reduction. In a paired placement-1 trial\n  a cascaded ALU/link/product mux reached 79.211 MHz. Independently masking\n  each result with its registered enable raised that to 81.110 MHz.\n  \n  The scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,238,429 bracketed\n  cycles with both bus stalls, saving 35,482 cycles. All five CRCs and the\n  validation banner matched. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 77.891,\n  81.110 and 81.110 MHz. The median gives 250.46 iterations/s, 4.68% above\n  239.26. The acceptance floor at this cycle count is 81.047 MHz, leaving\n  0.063 MHz of margin. Placement 0 used 3,945 LUT4s, 1,413 registers and\n  two DSPs. All 53 ALTOPS formal checks passed, and selftest and the three\n  random programs matched the reference with and without stalls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry eleven ordinary ALU result enables, a low and a high multiply\n      enable, and two multiply signedness flags into EX. Carry the high\n      product and the high-product, divide and ordinary writeback enables\n      in ex_mem_t.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      On the waiting-to-EX transfer register one enable for each ALU\n      operation ADD through LUI, qualified by \"'!is_jump. Derive the four\n      multiply controls from the decoded op on this same transfer. Use\n      alu_op > ALU_MUL for the late M-result interlock. The existing\n      trap-qualified nonload writer match forwards an adjacent low MUL.\n      Keep high-half multiplies and divisions interlocked on a nonzero rd\n      match. Hold the controls with their operands across backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Give the multiplier two explicit sign-extension inputs and a 64-bit\n      output. Multiply the two signed 33-bit concatenations. In ALTOPS\n      mode duplicate the opcode'\"'s specified 32-bit add/subtract and XOR\n      result into both halves so the same result-selection hardware applies.\n      Expose the ordinary ALU functions for one-hot result selection.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Multiply forwarded rs1 and rs2. The extra lhs bit is rs1[31] for\n      MULH and MULHSU, and the extra rhs bit is rs2[31] for MULH. Set the\n      other extension bits to zero. At the EX/MEM input form one bitwise\n      OR over individually enabled PC+4, product[31:0], ADD, SUB, AND, OR,\n      XOR, SLT, SLTU, SLL, SRL, SRA and LUI results. Use the existing ALU\n      operand selection for the eleven integer functions. Register the sum\n      of products in alu_result and product[63:32] in its own field on the\n      same EX/MEM enable. Keep effective_addr on its dedicated datapath.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Remove the MEM multiplier instance. OR four masked values for\n      collection and writeback. Select the registered high product on a\n      high multiply, div_result on a divide or remainder, formatted load\n      data on mem_to_reg, and EX/MEM.alu_result for the remaining results,\n      including low MUL. Capture that single selected value at MEM/WB\n      and retain it throughout a data wait.\n  - file: test/test_multiplier.py\n    description: |\n      Drive the two extension bits and check the appropriate product half\n      for every existing signed, mixed-sign, unsigned and low-product vector.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      In multiply_issue_and_dependencies require the ADD after low MUL to\n      retire on the next cycle. Keep the two-cycle gaps for consumers of\n      MULHU and MULHSU, the x0 case, and all RVFI operand/result checks.\n      Exercise low MUL as both operands of a consumer and as the source\n      of a store held across a data-bus stall.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,jsonschema,yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r10s0.yaml')\nobj=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(obj,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert obj['id']==p.stem\nprint(p,obj['category'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_71", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r10s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-001-r10s0\ntitle: Forward the low multiply product from EX/MEM\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  MUL pays the same adjacent-consumer bubble as MULH even though its low\n  32 bits fit the existing EX/MEM forwarding register. Gowin also traces\n  the champion's placement 0 setup limit from EX/MEM.alu_op[2] through\n  the MEM multiplier to a collected operand. In the median placement a\n  similar multiply path arrives only 0.007 ns before the limiting path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move the existing DSP multiply to EX. Give the low product a dedicated\n  enable in the ordinary EX result mux so the next instruction can use\n  it through the existing bypass. Capture the high product separately at\n  EX/MEM and select it in MEM for MULH, MULHU and MULHSU. This removes the\n  live DSP output from operand collection and saves the dependency bubble\n  on MUL. Decode the result enables in collection so adding the low product\n  costs one input to the EX result reduction. In a paired placement-1 trial\n  a cascaded ALU/link/product mux reached 79.211 MHz. Independently masking\n  each result with its registered enable raised that to 81.110 MHz.\n  \n  The scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,238,429 bracketed\n  cycles with both bus stalls, saving 35,482 cycles. All five CRCs and the\n  validation banner matched. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 77.891,\n  81.110 and 81.110 MHz. The median gives 250.46 iterations/s, 4.68% above\n  239.26. The acceptance floor at this cycle count is 81.047 MHz, leaving\n  0.063 MHz of margin. Placement 0 used 3,945 LUT4s, 1,413 registers and\n  two DSPs. All 53 ALTOPS formal checks passed, and selftest and the three\n  random programs matched the reference with and without stalls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry eleven ordinary ALU result enables, a low and a high multiply\n      enable, and two multiply signedness flags into EX. Carry the high\n      product and the high-product, divide and ordinary writeback enables\n      in ex_mem_t.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      On the waiting-to-EX transfer register one enable for each ALU\n      operation ADD through LUI, qualified by \"'!is_jump. Derive the four\n      multiply controls from the decoded op on this same transfer. Use\n      alu_op > ALU_MUL for the late M-result interlock. The existing\n      trap-qualified nonload writer match forwards an adjacent low MUL.\n      Keep high-half multiplies and divisions interlocked on a nonzero rd\n      match. Hold the controls with their operands across backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Give the multiplier two explicit sign-extension inputs and a 64-bit\n      output. Multiply the two signed 33-bit concatenations. In ALTOPS\n      mode duplicate the opcode'\"'s specified 32-bit add/subtract and XOR\n      result into both halves so the same result-selection hardware applies.\n      Expose the ordinary ALU functions for one-hot result selection.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Multiply forwarded rs1 and rs2. The extra lhs bit is rs1[31] for\n      MULH and MULHSU, and the extra rhs bit is rs2[31] for MULH. Set the\n      other extension bits to zero. At the EX/MEM input form one bitwise\n      OR over individually enabled PC+4, product[31:0], ADD, SUB, AND, OR,\n      XOR, SLT, SLTU, SLL, SRL, SRA and LUI results. Use the existing ALU\n      operand selection for the eleven integer functions. Register the sum\n      of products in alu_result and product[63:32] in its own field on the\n      same EX/MEM enable. Keep effective_addr on its dedicated datapath.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Remove the MEM multiplier instance. OR four masked values for\n      collection and writeback. Select the registered high product on a\n      high multiply, div_result on a divide or remainder, formatted load\n      data on mem_to_reg, and EX/MEM.alu_result for the remaining results,\n      including low MUL. Capture that single selected value at MEM/WB\n      and retain it throughout a data wait.\n  - file: test/test_multiplier.py\n    description: |\n      Drive the two extension bits and check the appropriate product half\n      for every existing signed, mixed-sign, unsigned and low-product vector.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      In multiply_issue_and_dependencies require the ADD after low MUL to\n      retire on the next cycle. Keep the two-cycle gaps for consumers of\n      MULHU and MULHSU, the x0 case, and all RVFI operand/result checks.\n      Exercise low MUL as both operands of a consumer and as the source\n      of a store held across a data-bus stall.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,jsonschema,yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r10s0.yaml')\nobj=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(obj,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert obj['id']==p.stem\nprint(p,obj['category'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r10s0.yaml micro_opt\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_72","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'd=$(cat \"$TMPDIR/r10-scratch-path\")\ncat > \"$d/link.py\" <<'\"'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport shutil\nd=Path(__file__).parent; rtl=d/'link'; shutil.copytree(d/'onehot',rtl)\ndef edit(n,old,new):\n p=rtl/(n+'.sv'); s=p.read_text(); assert s.count(old)==1,(n,old); p.write_text(s.replace(old,new))\nedit('core_pkg','    logic [10:0] result_sel;','    logic [31:0] pc_plus4;\\\\n    logic [10:0] result_sel;')\nedit('core','    collected = waiting;', '    collected = waiting;\\\\n    collected.pc_plus4 = waiting.pc + 32\\\\'d4;')\np=rtl/'ex_stage.sv';s=p.read_text().replace(\\\"in.pc + 32'd4\\\",\\\"in.pc_plus4\\\");p.write_text(s)\nPY\npython \\\"\"'$d/link.py\"\nPYTHONPATH=. python \"$d/timing.py\" link 1'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
