{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_97", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r10s0.yaml\nd=$(cat \"$TMPDIR/r10-scratch-path\"); tail -2 \"$d/clean-p2/gw.log\"; tail -2 \"$d/final-p1/gw.log\"; date -u'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-001-r10s0\ntitle: Forward the low multiply product from EX/MEM\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  MUL pays the same adjacent-consumer bubble as MULH even though its low\n  32 bits fit the existing EX/MEM forwarding register. Gowin also traces\n  the champion's placement 0 setup limit from EX/MEM.alu_op[2] through\n  the MEM multiplier to a collected operand. In the median placement a\n  similar multiply path arrives only 0.007 ns before the limiting path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move the existing DSP multiply to EX. Give the low product a dedicated\n  enable in the ordinary EX result mux so the next instruction can use\n  it through the existing bypass. Capture the high product separately at\n  EX/MEM and select it in MEM for MULH, MULHU and MULHSU. This removes the\n  live DSP output from operand collection and saves the dependency bubble\n  on MUL. Decode the result enables in collection so adding the low product\n  costs one input to the EX result reduction. In paired placement-1 trials\n  the cascaded ALU/link/product mux reached 79.211 MHz. Forming the eleven\n  masked integer terms inside the ALU, then ORing its output with the enabled\n  PC+4 and low product in EX reached 83.677 MHz.\n  \n  The scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,238,429 bracketed\n  cycles with both bus stalls, saving 35,482 cycles. All five CRCs and the\n  validation banner matched. The qualifying median at this cycle count\n  is 81.047 MHz. Placement 1 projects 258.39 iterations/s, 7.99% above\n  the champion. All six cocotb suites passed and selftest and the three\n  random programs matched the reference with and without bus stalls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry eleven ordinary ALU result enables, a low and a high multiply\n      enable, and two multiply signedness flags into EX. Carry the high\n      product and the high-product, divide and ordinary writeback enables\n      in ex_mem_t.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Drive the co\n…[2397 of 5469 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:26ed56d07f4a242f]…\nve the one-hot function select alongside each ordinary ALU vector. Continue checking all integer and divide results.\n  - file: test/test_multiplier.py\n    description: |\n      Drive the two extension bits and check the appropriate product half\n      for every existing signed, mixed-sign, unsigned and low-product vector.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      In consumers_that_still_need_wb require a one-cycle gap at PC 16\n      (MUL to BNE) and PC 28 (MUL to LW). Retain two cycles at PCs 4,\n      32 and 44. In multiply_issue_and_dependencies require the ADD after low MUL to\n      retire on the next cycle. Keep the two-cycle gaps for consumers of\n      MULHU and MULHSU, the x0 case, and all RVFI operand/result checks.\n      Exercise low MUL as both operands of a consumer and as the source\n      of a store held across a data-bus stall.\n[65%] Tech-Mapping Phase 1 completed\n[75%] Tech-Mapping Phase 2 completed\n[25%] Optimizing Phase 2 completed\nRunning inference ...\nSat Nov 21 19:17:55 UTC 2026\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
