{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r1s0 (regression, +42.82%): Testing 15 divisor multiples in parallel raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 7.774 MHz, but chaining eight radix-16 remainder selections in one EX cycle kept the core below the multicycle divider's 43.9 MHz.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r1s1 (improvement, +705.78%): Registering the shared EX divider after every six restoring steps shortened the critical path, and CoreMark incurred no divide stalls because it executed no DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU between timing markers.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Predicting branches with 32 two-bit counters and steering JALs in fetch avoided one-cycle EX redirects and cut CoreMark cycles by 6.4%, but the combinational EX divider kept Fmax near 5.5 MHz versus 43.9 MHz with multicycle division.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r2s0 (regression, +8.63%): Testing D, 2D and 3D in parallel cut each six-bit divider round from six dependent remainder selections to three, raising Fmax until a path from the register file set the limit.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r2s1 (regression, +5.00%): Splitting at divisor magnitude 2^14 raised Fmax at the same six-round latency because small divisors need only 15-bit trial subtractors and seeding the wide remainder with the top 14 dividend bits cuts the 33-bit subtractor chain from six to three per round.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r2s2 (improvement, +9.91%): Storing the control-transfer instruction word in the 64-entry BTB let a tag hit satisfy fetch during imem stalls even when the predictor chose fall-through, saving 154,266 CoreMark cycles over the same BTB with replay disabled.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r3s1 (regression, +12.14%): Replaying ordinary instructions from a 256-word backedge region during imem stalls saved 504,231 CoreMark cycles over BTB-only replay, while the round winner could start replay on either branch outcome and gained 14.0% throughput.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r3s2 (improvement, +14.00%): Separate 16-word sequences at each BTB index \n…[5457 of 8529 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c0e0e873d7fd7805]…\ntion in CoreMark cycles could not offset its lower median Fmax relative to the round winner (72.784 vs 78.330 MHz).\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r10s0 (improvement, +7.69%): Forwarding the EX multiplier's low 32 bits through the existing EX/MEM bypass eliminated the stall for an adjacent MUL consumer, while registering the high half separately for MEM kept the DSP off the operand collection path.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r10s1 (regression, +6.25%): Capturing high-product sign bits in collection required an interlock on adjacent writers to avoid stale signs, while deriving those bits from forwarded EX operands reached a higher median Fmax (83.439 vs 82.325 MHz) at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r10s2 (regression, +6.64%): Replaying words from CoreMark’s dominant 4 KiB data page bypassed bus waits and saved 165,297 cycles over ten iterations in the prototype, while forwarding the replay RAM output through a dependent BEQ/BNE to fetch limited an early placement to 73.508 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
