{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'hyp-20261121-00[123]-r(9|10|11)s' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"23:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r9s0 (regression, +6.62%): Masking wrong-path replay hits with `recovering` took the load-dependent redirect off the replay resets but left the ALU-to-RAM address path critical at 71.917 MHz median, while parallel address sums, byte-lane load comparisons and reuse of the divider’s final lane registers raised the median to 76.247 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n24:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r9s1 (improvement, +9.54%): Registering per-source bypass enables during producer EX removed `EX/MEM.mem_read` from the next address calculation, and comparing active RAM bytes in parallel with an extension check on the other operand let dependent BEQ/BNE resolve without waiting for load formatting.\n25:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r9s2 (regression, +5.88%): Looking up load words during operand collection let adjacent branches and pointer loads use a registered operand in EX while MEM verified the hit, but the prototype’s 4.1% reduction in CoreMark cycles could not offset its lower median Fmax relative to the round winner (72.784 vs 78.330 MHz).\n26:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r10s0 (improvement, +7.69%): Forwarding the EX multiplier's low 32 bits through the existing EX/MEM bypass eliminated the stall for an adjacent MUL consumer, while registering the high half separately for MEM kept the DSP off the operand collection path.\n27:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r10s1 (regression, +6.25%): Capturing high-product sign bits in collection required an interlock on adjacent writers to avoid stale signs, while deriving those bits from forwarded EX operands reached a higher median Fmax (83.439 vs 82.325 MHz) at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n28:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r10s2 (regression, +6.64%): Replaying words from CoreMark’s dominant 4 KiB data page bypassed bus waits and saved 165,297 cycles over ten iterations in the prototype, while forwarding the replay RAM output through a dependent BEQ/BNE to fetch limited an early placement to 73.508 MHz.\n29:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r11s0 (regression, +1.87%): After operand collection absorbed PC and immediate selection and radix-4 trials shortened divider feedback, the path from `bypass_rs1_q` through the multiplier DSP to the registered low product limited median Fmax to 84.996 MHz.\n30:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r11s1 (regression, +4.21%): Replacing the five restoring lanes with a reciprocal lookup and two uses of the EX multiplier made the path from data RAM to the fetch recovery PC critical at 86.950 MHz median, with CoreMark still taking 3,238,429 cycles.\n31:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r11s2 (improvement, +8.37%): Forwarding a resident-page word predicted during operand collection through EX/MEM removed the adjacent load-use bubble on a verified hit, while a second probe at the forwarded EX address could recover from a stale base guess without a bus read.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
