{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_81","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r13s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-001-r13s0\ntitle: Forward integer sums from the registered address adder\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 92.229 MHz placement has a 16.137 ns path from a saved\n  operand to EX/MEM.alu_result[29]. It crosses the ALU carry chain and\n  then seven result-selection LUTs. This path is within 0.004 ns of the\n  worst setup path. EX already has a full-width effective-address register\n  and the value of that register is free to use on an integer ADD or ADDI.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use one EX adder for integer addition, memory addresses and JALR targets.\n  Prepare its right operand in collection. The validated alu_src bit\n  selects the immediate or collected rs2 value. Keep the adjacent-rs2\n  bypass enabled only for a register operand. The left operand is the\n  ordinary forwarded rs1. For AUIPC, collection already registers the full\n  PC + U-immediate sum in direct_target_q.\n  \n  Capture the shared sum directly in EX/MEM.mem_addr. For ADD and ADDI\n  merge that registered word into the ordinary result with a registered\n  one-bit enable. This puts the EX/MEM boundary immediately after the\n  carry chain and preserves next-cycle forwarding. Select all other result\n  classes on their existing paths. Use the original post-forward rs2 for\n  store data and RVFI, including when the adder uses an immediate.\n  \n  On CoreMark the acceptance floor is 96.473 MHz at 3,044,309 marker\n  cycles. The gain depends on whether shortening the carry path and\n  removing a duplicated adder outweigh the additional gate on the\n  registered forwarding bus.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture the selected ALU right operand at the collection-to-EX edge.\n      On a failed adjacent-load prediction update it along with rs2 only\n      when bypass_alu_b_q selected the failed value. Hold it across memory\n      waits. Exclude AUIPC from the integer result selects and remove the\n      PC-specific first-operand bypass enable.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose the full 32-bit a+b sum as an output and use that same wire\n      for the module's standalone ADD operation.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Feed the ALU from forwarded rs1 and the prepared right operand with\n      its qualified bypass. Use the sum for effective_addr and jalr_sum.\n      Mask ADD off at the integer ALU result-select port. On the EX/MEM\n      transfer register the ADD select beside mem_addr. OR the selected\n      registered sum into the value used by all alu_result consumers.\n      Select the existing branch_target for AUIPC writeback. Preserve the\n      stall enables, x0 rules, trap suppression and RVFI memory masks.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r13s0.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('valid')\nPY\ndate -u\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
