{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_99","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r13s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-001-r13s0\ntitle: Register adjacent bypass matches before EX and interlock a faulting writer\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  On the champion's 92.229 MHz Gowin placement, a path from bit 0 of\n  EX/MEM.alu_result to bit 24 of the next collected rs1 arrives at\n  16.141 ns. It traverses the forwarded base, the low address adder,\n  alignment checking and ex_writer before selecting a 32-bit operand.\n  Other destinations on that path include collected rs2. The tag match\n  and the producer's decoded write intent are both known a cycle earlier.\nhypothesis: |\n  Record the two adjacent RAW matches when a producer moves from\n  collection into EX and the following instruction moves from decode into\n  collection. For each source compare its decode address to the producer's\n  nonzero rd and qualify the match with producer validity and decoded\n  reg_write. Drive operand selection from those registered matches and EX\n  validity. This removes address generation and trap checking from the\n  path that zeros the saved value and selects the adjacent bypass.\n  \n  A load or link instruction can still lose its write permission in EX.\n  If its data or link alignment check fails and a waiting source matches,\n  use the existing late-result interlock for one issue bubble. Let the\n  producer advance with the trap and its architectural write suppressed.\n  The waiting consumer then collects the youngest surviving older value.\n  Redirects keep priority. Ordinary aligned producers, including loads\n  with a verified prediction, retain their adjacent bypass timing.\n  \n  Ten CoreMark iterations take 3,044,309 marker cycles on the champion.\n  The target is a median above 96.473 MHz at that cycle count. Independent\n  ALU and redirect paths nearly tie the current forwarding path, so the\n  size of the placement gain is uncertain.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Register rs1 and rs2 matches from waiting.rd to the two decode read\n      addresses on joint advance. Hold both across a full stall. Clear\n      them on reset, redirect or a result-hazard bubble that advances EX\n      while holding decode. Feed the existing collection masks and bypass\n      enable registers from these matches and producer write intent.\n      Classify a faulting writer as a late result at the hazard-unit input.\n      Keep failed load-prediction repair and WB updates during holds.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Supply valid decoded register-write intent as bypass_writer. Export\n      a separate fault signal for a valid decoded writer with link_fault\n      or data_fault. Continue using the exact alignment checks for EX/MEM\n      reg_write, memory controls, traps and redirect targets.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      In misaligned_load_preserves_older_writer, require a two-cycle\n      retirement interval between the faulting LW and its dependent ADD.\n      Retain the exact older x6 value on both sources, the suppressed\n      destination and bus effects, and the retirement PC sequence.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,jsonschema,yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r13s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert all((Path('cores/bench') / f['file']).is_file() for f in d['changes']);print('schema and referenced paths valid')\nPY\ndate -u\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
