{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r.get('round_id') in [12,13]: print(r['id'], r['title'],r.get('critical_path'),r['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis'),r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r12s0 Resolve trailing quotient-bit pairs in the three narrow divider lanes {'from': 'cpu/id_ex_w.pc_1_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_29_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.954, 'arrival_ns': 17.252, 'logic_cells': 39, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']} Resolve the trailing quotient-bit pairs of lanes 0, 1 and 2 with\nradix-4 restoring trials. In each round, consume bits from the MSB\nin groups of (1,2,2) for the 4-bit lane, (2,2) for the 11-bit lane,\nand (1,2) for the 18-bit lane. Each pair computes three candidate\ndifferences concurrently. A balanced two-level mux selects the restored\nremainder. This cuts the dependent selections from five to three in\nlane 0 and from three to two in lanes 1 and 2. Lane 3 uses its two\nbinary trials and lane 4 uses one. A shared 20-bit triple of the low\n18 divisor-magnitude bits suffices for all three paired lanes.\nThe scratch prototype reached 85.422, 92.638 and 92.638 MHz on Gowin\nplacements 0, 1 and 2. At the 92.638 MHz median it scored 304.30\nCoreMark iter/s, 8.99% above 279.21. It matched all five canonical\nCRCs in 3,044,309 marker cycles and passed all 23 ALU tests.\n \nhyp-20261121-002-r12s1 Divide small divisors by table reciprocal and large divisors one octal digit per clock {'from': 'cpu/bypass_rs1_q_s1', 'to': 'cpu/id_ex_w.rs2_val_30_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.056, 'arrival_ns': 18.35, 'logic_cells': 11, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu']} Replace the five restoring lanes with two exact unsigned algorithms split\nat divisor magnitude 2048. Capture magnitudes A and D on div_start. For\n1 <= D < 2048, read M = floor((2^32 - 1)/D) from a synchronous 2048 by\n32-bit constant ROM. A private registered-input multiplier computes\nQ0 = high32(A*M), then P = Q0*D. The reciprocal is at most one unit below\n2^32/D, so for 32-bit A the estimate is exact or one low. Round one reads\nM. Round two registers A and M at the multiplier inputs, round three\ncaptures Q0, and round four registers Q0 and D at the same inputs. Round\nfive captures the low wor\n…[8290 of 11362 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3f4b948fd191b575]…\ne of rd through normal register read and forwarding.\nA misaligned store takes the same recovery path. The faulting\ninstruction advances once with its original trap and suppressed side\neffects. Keep ctrl_with_trap.reg_write on the MEM and architectural\nwrite paths. The existing advancing gate prevents a held or\nload-repairing instruction from initiating recovery.\n\nA scratch prototype made those two EX control changes. It completed\nten iterations in 3,044,309 marker cycles with both stall generators,\nall five canonical CRCs and the validation banner. Gowin placements\n0, 1 and 2 reached 94.763, 97.260 and 97.260 MHz. The median gives\n319.48 iterations/s, 5.45% above the champion. The design used\n4,883 logic cells, 1,934 registers, 25 BSRAMs and two DSPs. Selftest\nand all three random programs matched the ISS with and without bus\nstalls. The prototype passed all 53 single-retirement ALTOPS formal\nchecks and all 46 pipeline tests, including the fault-recovery timing\nchecks and stalled-load case described below.\n \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
