nstructions from a 256-word backedge region during imem stalls saved 504,231 CoreMark cycles over BTB-only replay, while the round winner could start replay on either branch outcome and gained 14.0% throughput.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r3s2 (improvement, +14.00%): Separate 16-word sequences at each BTB index \n…[6320 of 9392 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fd4a13641a69b2e5]…\nhrough six full-width restoring steps. This limits every\\ninstruction to 42.974 MHz even though CoreMark executes zero div\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261121-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Bypass adjacent loads into equality branches and pointer-load address generation\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion spends 3,548,404 cycles on ten CoreMark iterations. Between\\nthe markers, 134,051 load interlocks precede BEQ or BNE, and another\\n59,251 match the base register of the next load. Both consumers can use a\\ndedicated MEM-to-EX operand path. Feeding the entire EX ALU from load data\\ninstead cost a scratch prototype nearly 6 MHz.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion spends 3,548,404 cycles on ten CoreMark iterations. Between\\nthe markers, 134,051 load interlocks precede BEQ or BNE, and another\\n59,251 match the base register of the next load. Both consumers can use a\\ndedicated MEM-to-EX operand path. Feeding the entire EX ALU from load data\\ninstead cost a scratch prototype nearly 6 MHz.\\n\", \"hypothesi\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
