{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor x in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if x['id'].endswith(('r4s0','r3s0','r2s0','r2s1')):print(x['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{'motivation': \"Gowin traced the champion's 197-cell worst path from quotient_q[35] to\\nremainder_q[12]. Each divider round puts six dependent trial subtractions\\nand remainder selections between those registers. CoreMark executed zero\\ndivides or remainders between the markers, yet this path sets the clock\\nfor all 4,491,485 measured cycles. At that cycle count, 45.89 MHz would\\nclear the acceptance margin over the champion's 43.865 MHz.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Replace each pair of binary restoring steps with one radix-4 digit.\\nFor each digit, append two dividend bits to the partial remainder and\\nsubtract D, 2D and 3D in parallel, where D is the divisor magnitude.\\nUse the borrow flags to select the largest multiple that fits, its\\ntwo-bit digit, and its already-computed residual. Resolve the top two\\ndividend bits in round one and six bits in each of the next five rounds.\\nKeep the current six-round protocol in hardware and ALTOPS.\\n\\nA scratch RTL prototype reached 46.988, 47.706 and 47.706 MHz on Gowin\\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2. CoreMark validated all five CRCs in\\n4,491,485 bracketed cycles for ten iterations. The measured median gives\\n106.21 iterations/s, 8.76% above the 97.66 champion. Option 0 used\\n3,048 LUT4s against the champion's 2,756. All three worst paths now\\nstart at the register file.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 9, 'confidence': 'high'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'Keep operand capture, sign correction and the existing round counter.\\nUse a 32-bit combined dividend/quotient shift register initialized to\\nthe dividend magnitude. Use round one to register 3D from the captured\\ndivisor magnitude and resolve the top two dividend bits.\\nThe first digit uses a small decode for D equal to 0, 1, 2, 3 or\\ngreater than 3. Shift in that first digit by two bits. In the remaining\\nfive rounds shift in the three successive digits by six bits.\\nUse 34-bit unsigned trials and multiples with an explicit borrow bit.\\nSelect residuals one-hot so digit encoding is off the r\n…[8264 of 11336 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:66003b07f5cf045c]…\n captured mode to write the selected residual to R. Shift Q\\nleft by six and insert either six narrow quotient bits or three zeros\\nfollowed by the three wide bits. After six rounds the narrow quotient\\nis Q[31:0]. Gather the low three bits of each six-bit group in order\\nfor the wide quotient. Fill its upper 14 bits with copies of D==0.\\nFor D=0 the wide residual reconstructs A and every quotient bit is one.\\nApply the existing result selection and sign correction. Retain reset,\\ndone/accept behavior, INT_MIN/-1 semantics and all ALTOPS equations.\\n\"}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Check DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU against the integer oracle on both\\nsides of D=0x4000, including negative signed operands and transitions\\nthrough D=0. At D=0x4000 use dividends q*D+r with a different octal\\ndigit in each of the six quotient groups. Exercise each group alone\\nand r in {0,1,D-1} to catch reversed groups, dropped bits and borrow\\nerrors. Retain the exact six-round deadline and held-result checks.\\n'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
