{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_140","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r7s0.yaml' <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-001-r7s0\ntitle: Share one synchronous instruction-word RAM across both fetch replay sources\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion spent 179,329 of its 3,399,350 CoreMark marker cycles waiting\n  for an instruction while decode was free and EX was not redirecting.\n  Fetch selects among the external bus, a 32-bit word in each BTB entry,\n  and a separately tagged successor word. Storing the instruction once by\n  word PC removes a wide mux input and lets a trained word serve a wait\n  after any predecessor, including beyond the sixteenth sequential slot.\nhypothesis: |\n  Factor instruction storage out of the existing 64-entry BTB. Give\n  controls and ordinary instructions one 4,096-row synchronous replay RAM\n  indexed by PC[13:2]. Each 39-bit row holds the instruction, PC[19:14]\n  and a valid bit. Preserve the BTB indices, targets, two-bit direction\n  counters and EX training policy. The same EX write port can install\n  either kind of word. Read the held PC or the predicted next index on\n  the PC update edge. A redirect cancels that read. A ready external\n  word takes priority, including on recovery.\n  \n  With separate BTB instruction words and ordinary-word RAM at this depth,\n  a scratch implementation took 3,245,848 cycles at 56.012 MHz on placement\n  1. Sharing the word RAM took 3,235,356 cycles. Gowin placements 0, 1\n  and 2 reached 56.111, 58.147 and 58.147 MHz, giving 179.72 iterations/s\n  at the median, 5.46% above 170.41. The ten-iteration run passed all five\n  CRCs under instruction and data stalls. At this cycle count the acceptance\n  threshold is 57.67 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Remove the BTB word array and per-path successor slots. Write a packed\n      row on update.write or update.successor. Qualify a synchronous response\n      with the stored upper tag, aligned low-RAM fetch PC, and a registered\n      read/write collision and recovery check. A blocked fetch retries its\n      current row. On reset or FENCE.I, suppress replay immediately and\n      sweep all 4,096 row-valid bits through the write port, one per cycle.\n      Restart the sweep on each invalidation. External fetch and retirement\n      continue during the sweep. Gate invalid responses to a bubble.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise cold fetch during the sweep, warm the shared RAM before\n      forcing replay, and check immediate invalidation on FENCE.I. Update\n      the expected fetch cycles for the seventeenth word and recovery.\n      Exercise aliases 0x4000 bytes apart and same-row read/write collisions.\n      Verify exact retired PCs, instructions, write data and store counts\n      across each hold, override and redirect.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nprint(170.41*1.046*3235356/1e7)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
