{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r7s1','r8s0','r8s2')): print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],r['critical_path'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-002-r7s1 Give each of 2,048 instruction rows an explicit predicted successor.\nStore PC+4 for an ordinary instruction or a predicted fall-through, and\nthe resolved target for a predicted-taken control transfer. On the fast\npath the registered row supplies the next RAM index directly. Keep the\narchitectural fetch PC independent and check the returned row against\nit before using either the instruction or its prediction.\n\nWhen decode stalls on a verified row, save the current row in a one-entry\nbuffer. The RAM can finish the successor read on that same edge and hold\nits output until decode consumes the buffered instruction. Both words\nthen issue in order without another lookup. This handles a dependency\nor a data-bus wait without feeding the hazard signal into the read-address\nmux. At the issue boundary, a registered valid bit kills wrong-path work\nwithout routing EX recovery through every operand and control register.\n\nA scratch prototype completed the canonical ten-iteration run in 3,223,900\nmarker cycles with both bus stalls, saving 175,450 cycles. Gowin placement\noptions 0, 1 and 2 reached 53.865, 58.567 and 58.567 MHz. The median gives\n181.67 iterations/s, 6.60 percent above 170.41. At this cycle count the\nacceptance threshold is 57.466 MHz. Option 0 used 3,821 LUT4s, 1,158\nregisters, 15 BSRAMs and two DSPs. Placement remains the main uncertainty.\n\nThe prototype passed all 53 ALTOPS formal checks. Selftest and three\nrandom programs matched the reference retirement traces under bus stalls.\nThe scratch cocotb suite passed 99 cases, including an added 8 KiB alias\ntest. Four fetch-visit expectations were updated for the new lookup timing.\nAll architectural result, trap and store checks passed.\n {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.rd_1_s0', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -12.506, 'arrival_ns': 22.801, 'logic_cells': 17, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nhyp-20261121-001-r8s0 Match each decoded source against the destinations in EX, MEM and WB\non the edge tha\n…[2212 of 5284 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b163ae0aa422e662]…\n\nverified prediction can supply the same word and complete the load.\nA wrong carry or tag costs the usual memory wait. Full-word stores\ninstall their data and partial stores invalidate the indexed word.\n\nA scratch RTL prototype took 3,078,813 marker cycles versus 3,223,900\nfor the champion with both stall generators, all five canonical CRCs\nand the validation banner. This saves 145,087 cycles. It needs median\nGowin Fmax above 60.6763 MHz to clear 197.07686 iterations/s. That\nnarrow timing margin is the main risk. The version using the general\nALU result for the external address reached 59.588 MHz median and\n193.54 iterations/s. Its worst path on placement 1 ran from a WB\nregister identifier through EX forwarding and the ALU to data RAM.\nRegistering the corrected low address separately targets that path.\n {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.rd_1_s0', 'to': 'dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -13.267, 'arrival_ns': 23.562, 'logic_cells': 24, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
