{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r5s0','r6s0','r5s1','r7s0')): print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r5s0 Release the adjacent consumer for two cases. BEQ and BNE can compare the\nformatted MEM load value against the other forwarded operand. A load whose\nbase comes from an aligned LW can add its immediate to raw dmemRData in\nparallel with the ordinary EX address calculation. Select this sum at the\nEX/MEM register. Keep load data off the general ALU and JALR target paths.\nTo bound the resulting load-to-fetch path, a redirect kills the outstanding\nsuccessor replay read. Only a subsequently accepted BTB control restarts\nreplay. The PC still redirects immediately and EX still trains the BTB.\nThis trades some fetch coverage after mispredictions for a shorter\nload-to-branch path into the replay RAM.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,399,350 bracketed cycles\nwith both bus stalls enabled and all five canonical CRCs. The acceptance\nfrequency at that cycle count is 49.61 MHz. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2\nreached 51.040, 51.193 and 51.193 MHz, yielding 150.60 iterations/s at\nthe median, 7.94% above the champion. With immediate resolved-path\nreplay the same bypass took 3,353,834 cycles but reached only 46.940 MHz\nat the median. Selftest and all three random programs matched the\nreference retirement trace under both bus stalls.\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r5s1 Schedule the single data port one edge ahead. An older store or an\noutstanding load gets the registered address for its MEM cycle. When\nthat slot is free, decode can prepare an effective address for a younger\nload and register a grant beside ID/EX. In EX, a valid granted load to\naligned ordinary RAM can complete on the ready handshake. Its immediate\nconsumer then issues and takes the registered result from EX/MEM.\nOtherwise the load follows the existing MEM and load-use interlock.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten CoreMark iterations in 3,288,627\nmarker cycles with both stall generators and all five canonical CRCs.\nThis saves 259,777 cycles versus the champion. Gowin placements 0, 1\nand 2 reached 48.382, 49.029 and 49.029 MHz. The median yie\n…[1509 of 4581 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e1c058b26aaa0225]…\nit. Preserve the BTB indices, targets, two-bit direction\ncounters and EX training policy. The same EX write port can install\neither kind of word. Read the held PC or the predicted next index on\nthe PC update edge. A redirect cancels that read. A ready external\nword takes priority, including on recovery.\n\nWith separate BTB instruction words and ordinary-word RAM at this depth,\na scratch implementation took 3,245,848 cycles at 56.012 MHz on placement\n1. Sharing the word RAM took 3,235,356 cycles. Gowin placements 0, 1\nand 2 reached 56.111, 58.147 and 58.147 MHz, giving 179.72 iterations/s\nat the median, 5.47% above 170.41. The ten-iteration run passed all five\nCRCs under instruction and data stalls. At this cycle count the acceptance\nthreshold is 57.67 MHz. Selftest and all three random programs matched\nthe reference retirement trace with both stalls enabled. All 31 pipeline\ncases passed in a scratch fixture that allowed the validity sweep to\nfinish before testing warm replay and checked the new fetch cycles.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
