{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_100", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r10s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-002-r10s1\ntitle: Forward the low multiply result from EX/MEM\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark executes 93,960 MUL instructions between its timing markers. The\n  champion computes every product in MEM, so an adjacent consumer waits one\n  cycle. The placement-0 critical path also crosses that multiplier on its\n  way from the registered opcode to a collected operand. The low 32 product\n  bits are identical for signed and unsigned operands. MUL can use the same\n  adjacent-result bypass as ADD, even when its own input was just produced.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move one shared full-width multiplier into EX. Give its two halves\n  different destinations at the EX/MEM edge. Merge the low half for MUL,\n  the link value for a jump, and the ordinary integer result with mutually\n  exclusive masks into the existing ALU-result register. That register\n  already reaches both inputs of the next EX instruction. Capture the high\n  half in a separate register and select it in MEM for MULH, MULHSU and\n  MULHU. This removes the DSP from the MEM-to-collection path and lets an\n  adjacent low-product consumer advance immediately.\n\n  Use the normal forwarded rs1 and rs2 for the 32 data bits on each DSP\n  input. Prepare the two signed extension bits in operand collection. A\n  high-product instruction waits if the immediately older EX instruction\n  writes either source, so its saved sign bits and operands agree. MUL\n  always uses zero extension and can consume an adjacent ordinary result\n  or low product directly. Loads, high products and divides retain their\n  existing interlocks as producers.\n\n  An isolated prototype completed ten iterations in 3,238,429 marker cycles,\n  down from 3,273,911, with both bus stalls, all five canonical CRCs and the\n  validation banner. Its stalled selftest and three randomized RVFI traces\n  matched the ISS. At this cycle count the acceptance floor is 81.047 MHz.\n  Gowin placement 1 reached 82.325 MHz, equivalent to 254.21 iterations/s.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the two operand extension bits to the EX input bundle and a\n      32-bit upper product to EX/MEM. The low product uses alu_result.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      On collection advance, latch rs1[31] qualified by MULH or MULHSU,\n      and rs2[31] qualified by MULH, from the youngest collected values.\n      Hold these bits with their instruction. Interlock high-product inputs\n      on a valid adjacent writer to a matching nonzero source. Classify MUL\n      as an ordinary EX producer. Keep the late-result interlock for upper\n      products, DIV/REM and the existing load-use cases.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose both halves of a signed 33-by-33 product of the prepared input\n      operands. The low 64 bits suffice for every RV32M multiply. Supply\n      the existing per-op ALTOPS value on the half used by that instruction.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Feed the multiplier from the two forwarded register operands and\n      their collected sign bits. On EX/MEM advance capture product[63:32]\n      separately. Form alu_result as a masked OR of product[31:0] enabled\n      by MUL, PC+4 enabled by is_jump, and the integer ALU output enabled by\n      a base ALU op and \"'!is_jump. Hold both registers through backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the registered upper product for the three high-product opcodes.\n      MUL already travels on in.alu_result. Use the same selected value for\n      MEM forwarding and MEM/WB writeback.\n  - file: test/test_multiplier.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt the existing full-width randomized and edge-case oracle checks\n      to the split result ports and explicit input sign bits. Check both\n      signedness choices against the same low product.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Require consecutive retirement for MUL feeding a branch, load base,\n      arithmetic operand or another MUL. Check the one-cycle wait on each\n      high-product dependency and the new wait when a high-product input\n      was just written. Exercise both source positions, double matches, x0,\n      mixed-sign inputs, dmem holds and a flushed multiply. Check RVFI\n      operands, values and order alongside the latency assertions.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r10s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint(d['title'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
