{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_78", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r11s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-002-r11s1\ntitle: \\\"Reuse the EX multiplier for exact reciprocal-based division\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  All three round-ten timing reports end in divider lane 1. The champion's\n  divisor-to-remainder path crosses 48 cells, although CoreMark executes no\n  divides between its markers. The EX multiplier is idle for all seven\n  rounds of a divide because that instruction owns EX. It can compute and\n  verify a quotient estimate within the existing retirement schedule.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace all five restoring lanes with a synchronous reciprocal ROM, a\n  small interpolation multiply, and two uses of the existing EX multiplier.\n  Capture unsigned magnitudes A and D and the architectural sign and zero\n  flags on issue. For nonzero D let k = floor(log2(D)) and N = D << (31-k).\n  Round one reads ROM row i = N[30:21] and registers t = N[20:8] and k.\n  For j from 0 through 1024 define U(j) = ceil(2\"'^42/(1024+j)). Store\n  U(i)-1 in 32 bits and floor((U(i)-U(i+1))/32) in 17 bits. Generate these\n  coefficients from integer constants at elaboration. Round two multiplies\n  the slope by t. Round three subtracts the product shifted right by eight\n  from U(i) to obtain the 33-bit reciprocal M.\n  \n  Round four computes A*M. For M = 2''^32 the high word is A. Round five\n  forms E = high_word >> k. Round six computes P = E*D, retaining 33 bits.\n  If P>A, round seven returns E-1 and (A+D)-P. Otherwise it returns E and\n  A-P. Precompute A+D and E-1 before that last edge. Apply the saved sign\n  and zero-divisor rules to the selected quotient or remainder.\n  \n  One correction suffices. The chord of the convex reciprocal is an upper\n  bound. Rounding the endpoints up and the slope, fraction and correction\n  down preserves that bound. For k<=10 the divisor is exactly a table\n  endpoint and rounding adds less than one reciprocal unit. For k>=11 the\n  chord error is at most 1024 units, fraction truncation adds at most 512,\n  and slope and integer rounding add less than 34. The resulting quotient\n  error before flooring is below 1570/2048. Thus E is exact or one high.\n  \n  On rounds three and five, load the next two factors into the held EX\n  operand registers and clear their adjacent-bypass enables. These edges\n  are private to the divide. The ordinary MUL path can still accept and\n  forward a low product on consecutive cycles. Qualify its first operand\n  in collection so the DSP contributes zero to an ordinary integer result\n  and the low product can enter the EX result OR directly. The critical\n  experiment is whether removing the divider feedback and this output\n  gate raises the median above 87.28 MHz at 3,238,429 bracketed cycles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the seven rounds with a 1024 by 49-bit synchronous ROM.\n      Emit unsigned factors on rounds three and five, and sample the\n      external multiplier product on rounds four and six. Update final\n      result storage once on div_last and retain it on a hold. Preserve\n      the ALTOPS formulas, transfer timing, divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Give the two internal factor writes access to the held EX operand\n      registers on their scheduled edges. Clear the corresponding bypass\n      bits. For ordinary issue register the multiply-qualified first\n      operand and its qualified adjacent-writer select. Preserve normal\n      forwarding, interlocks, reset and stalled-instruction ownership.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Feed both scalar multiply and divide factor pairs to the existing\n      multiplier. Use unsigned extension on internal factors and preserve\n      scalar signed-high modes. Return its full product to the divider.\n      OR the low word into the integer result using the qualified input.\n      Preserve the original operands captured at div_start for RVFI.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Model the shared multiplier connection in the standalone ALU test,\n      latching each requested factor pair and supplying its product on\n      the following round. Exercise every exponent, table endpoints and\n      neighbors, exact multiples and dividends one either side of them.\n      Keep the full arithmetic oracle and backpressure checks.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Interleave signed and unsigned products with dependent divisions,\n      exercise a normal ADD immediately after divider use of the DSP, and\n      check precise RVFI operands, consecutive MUL forwarding and the\n      existing eight-cycle independent-divide spacing under bus waits.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,jsonschema,yaml\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r11s1.yaml')\nx=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(x,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint(x['title'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_78", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r11s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-002-r11s1\ntitle: \\\"Reuse the EX multiplier for exact reciprocal-based division\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  All three round-ten timing reports end in divider lane 1. The champion's\n  divisor-to-remainder path crosses 48 cells, although CoreMark executes no\n  divides between its markers. The EX multiplier is idle for all seven\n  rounds of a divide because that instruction owns EX. It can compute and\n  verify a quotient estimate within the existing retirement schedule.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace all five restoring lanes with a synchronous reciprocal ROM, a\n  small interpolation multiply, and two uses of the existing EX multiplier.\n  Capture unsigned magnitudes A and D and the architectural sign and zero\n  flags on issue. For nonzero D let k = floor(log2(D)) and N = D << (31-k).\n  Round one reads ROM row i = N[30:21] and registers t = N[20:8] and k.\n  For j from 0 through 1024 define U(j) = ceil(2\"'^42/(1024+j)). Store\n  U(i)-1 in 32 bits and floor((U(i)-U(i+1))/32) in 17 bits. Generate these\n  coefficients from integer constants at elaboration. Round two multiplies\n  the slope by t. Round three subtracts the product shifted right by eight\n  from U(i) to obtain the 33-bit reciprocal M.\n  \n  Round four computes A*M. For M = 2''^32 the high word is A. Round five\n  forms E = high_word >> k. Round six computes P = E*D, retaining 33 bits.\n  If P>A, round seven returns E-1 and (A+D)-P. Otherwise it returns E and\n  A-P. Precompute A+D and E-1 before that last edge. Apply the saved sign\n  and zero-divisor rules to the selected quotient or remainder.\n  \n  One correction suffices. The chord of the convex reciprocal is an upper\n  bound. Rounding the endpoints up and the slope, fraction and correction\n  down preserves that bound. For k<=10