{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r.get('id','').endswith(('r11s0','r12s0','r10s0')):\n  print(r['id'])\n  print(r['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis'))\n  print(r['changes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r10s0\nMove the existing DSP multiply to EX. Give the low product a dedicated\nenable in the ordinary EX result mux so the next instruction can use\nit through the existing bypass. Capture the high product separately at\nEX/MEM and select it in MEM for MULH, MULHU and MULHSU. This removes the\nlive DSP output from operand collection and saves the dependency bubble\non MUL. Decode the result enables in collection so adding the low product\ncosts one input to the EX result reduction. In paired placement-1 trials\nthe cascaded ALU/link/product mux reached 79.211 MHz. Forming the eleven\nmasked integer terms inside the ALU, then ORing its output with the enabled\nPC+4 and low product in EX reached 83.677 MHz.\n\nThe scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,238,429 bracketed\ncycles with both bus stalls, saving 35,482 cycles. All five CRCs and the\nvalidation banner matched. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 79.634,\n83.677 and 83.677 MHz. The 83.677 MHz median gives 258.39 iterations/s,\n7.99% above 239.26. The qualifying median is 81.047 MHz.\nPlacement 0 used 3,892 LUT4s, 1,413 registers and two DSPs.\nAll 53 ALTOPS formal checks and all six cocotb suites passed. Selftest\nand the three random programs matched the reference with and without stalls.\n\n[{'file': 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'description': 'Carry eleven ordinary ALU result enables, a low and a high multiply\\nenable, and two multiply signedness flags into EX. Carry the high\\nproduct and the high-product, divide and ordinary writeback enables\\nin ex_mem_t.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': 'Drive the collection-only fields to zero in the waiting payload. Collection assigns their decoded values on issue.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'On the waiting-to-EX transfer register one enable for each ALU\\noperation ADD through LUI, qualified by !is_jump. Derive the four\\nmultiply controls from the decoded op on this same transfer. Use\\nalu_op > ALU_MUL for the late M-result interlock. The existing\\ntrap-qualified nonload writer match forwar\n…[5803 of 8875 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bded7b3562f407da]…\nses one. A shared 20-bit triple of the low\n18 divisor-magnitude bits suffices for all three paired lanes.\nThe scratch prototype reached 85.422, 92.638 and 92.638 MHz on Gowin\nplacements 0, 1 and 2. At the 92.638 MHz median it scored 304.30\nCoreMark iter/s, 8.99% above 279.21. It matched all five canonical\nCRCs in 3,044,309 marker cycles and passed all 23 ALU tests.\n\n[{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': \"On div_start register 3*divisor_abs[17:0] in 20 bits using a\\nwidened shift and add. For each radix-4 pair in a W-bit lane, form\\nX = {R,next_two_bits} and subtract D, 2D and the low W+2 bits of\\nregistered 3D in parallel. Zero-extend before subtracting in W+3\\nbits. The top bit of each difference gives lt1, lt2 or lt3. Use\\nlt2 to select between (lt1 ? X : X-D) and (lt3 ? X-2D : X-3D),\\nretaining the low W bits. Append {!lt2, !lt3 || (lt2 && !lt1)}\\nto the quotient. Preserve each lane's prefix seed, seven-round\\nschedule, result hold, signed correction, divide-by-zero handling\\nand formal ALTOPS path.\\n\"}]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
