{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_129","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r14s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-002-r14s1\ntitle: Capture the adjacent producer directly in each consumer operand register\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion runs ten iterations in 3,044,309 cycles at 97.260 MHz.\n  Its placement-0 worst path starts at EX/MEM result bit 2 and ends at\n  decode valid. In placement 1, the path through the integer ALU and\n  the path from a source register to fetch recovery are 0.001 ns apart.\n  Both use EX operands assembled from a saved value and a shared\n  32-bit feedback bus. The round-13 collection experiment still put\n  this last source selection in front of the consumer's ALU.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move delivery of the immediately preceding result to the edge on which\n  the consumer enters EX. Give each source register its own result\n  selection. In parallel with producer execution, collection determines\n  whether that port takes the adjacent writer or its existing MEM, WB\n  or saved-register fallback. An adjacent match selects one producer\n  result class: integer ALU, link PC, low product or formatted speculative\n  load. Gate those classes separately for each matching source and OR\n  them with its masked fallback at the register input. This keeps the\n  producer's final result mux and a second forwarding mux out of series.\n  Both source ports can capture the same producer on one edge. EX can\n  start a dependent branch comparison, address sum or multiply directly\n  from the registered operand. The normal EX/MEM result still serves MEM.\n  \n  Use the current advance and write-intent rules. A dependent high\n  multiply or division waits for MEM. For an adjacent load, retain one\n  speculative-source bit per port. On a failed early lookup hold the\n  consumer, replace only those source values with the verified MEM value\n  when memory completes, then re-execute. An advancing alignment fault\n  uses the champion's flush and refetch. The expected cycle count is\n  3,044,309, requiring a median above 101.734 MHz to qualify. The main\n  timing risk is the producer-to-operand register path through the low\n  product or integer result selection.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Make the integer result, low product, link PC and formatted load\n      candidate available in parallel to the operand capture logic.\n      Keep the corresponding EX/MEM result for architectural writeback.\n      Drive rs1 and rs2 from the captured values for comparisons, address\n      generation, the DSP, stores and RVFI. Select PC and immediate by\n      operand role for the integer ALU.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Build one collection result per source from the four masked producer\n      classes and the existing youngest eligible fallback. Qualify the\n      producer's class enables with each adjacent match before merging\n      data. Capture on \"'!stall_ex. Retain the adjacent-load repair mask,\n      correct each matched port once on repair && !mem_wait, and preserve\n      redirect priority, x0 and the late-result interlock.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Follow MUL by an ADD that reads its result twice and require\n      consecutive retirements. Repeat with a cold LW held by dmemReady,\n      older writes to the same rd and both ADD sources matching the load.\n      Check the single accepted read, both corrected RVFI source values,\n      final sum and one retirement per instruction.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,jsonschema,yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r14s1.yaml')\nv=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(v,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint(v['title'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
