{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_83", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r4s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-002-r4s1\ntitle: Partition RV32M execution across registered arithmetic stages\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 28.440 ns setup path crosses 170 cells between divider\n  remainder registers. A scratch build that broke that feedback loop\n  reached 44.178 MHz. Its new limiting path went from an EX operand\n  address through forwarding and the multiply DSP to the data RAM.\n  Both paths constrain ordinary integer instructions. CoreMark executed\n  93,960 multiplies and zero divides between its timing markers, so\n  independent multiplies still need to issue on consecutive clocks.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give RV32M arithmetic register boundaries appropriate to when each\n  result is needed. Multiply the forwarded operands held in EX/MEM\n  during MEM and capture the product in MEM/WB. Use the load-use\n  interlock for a consumer immediately following a multiply. Independent\n  instructions can advance every cycle. Divide and remainder continue\n  to block EX for six arithmetic clocks, each of which now computes a\n  fixed group of quotient bits in a dedicated restoring section.\n\n  A scratch prototype validated all five CoreMark CRCs in 3,548,404\n  marker cycles, 36,684 more than the champion. Gowin placement options\n  0, 1 and 2 reached 43.837, 46.824 and 46.824 MHz. The median gives\n  131.96 iterations/s, 7.84% above 122.37. At that cycle count the\n  acceptance threshold is 45.42 MHz. Option 0 used 4,473 LUT4s and\n  two DSPs. Selftest and three random programs matched the reference\n  retirement trace with both bus stalls enabled.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Move the four multiply results to MEM. For division capture and\n      hold both operand magnitudes at issue. Consume 7, 6, 5, 5, 5 and\n      4 dividend bits MSB first in six feed-forward sections. At each\n      boundary register that section's quotient digits and remainder.\n      After k total bits, the remainder fits in k bits. Use k+1 bits\n      for each tria\n…[1130 of 4202 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b5dd602c6b1b0d8a]…\n   destination match, held fetch, ID bubble and MEM/WB bypass then\n      deliver the product to an adjacent consumer. Keep the data-memory\n      request enables tied to the decoded load and store controls.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Keep all divider timing, arithmetic, reset and result-hold checks.\n      Add divisors just below, at and above the five internal prefix\n      limits. Move the existing multiply vectors to instruction-level\n      checks at their new writeback point.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Run the relocated multiply vectors and full-width signed, unsigned\n      and mixed-sign reference vectors through all four instruction\n      encodings. Check consecutive independent products retire one cycle\n      apart and an adjacent dependent ALU, branch or store gets the WB\n      product. Include a stalled older load, a wrong-path multiply and a\n      younger writer to the same rd. Check source data, destination data\n      and order on RVFI channel 0.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
