{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_125","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r5s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261121-002-r5s1\ntitle: Register branch recovery and replay training at the EX/MEM boundary\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion completes ten CoreMark iterations in 3,548,404 cycles at\n  49.506 MHz. Its reported 25.667 ns path starts at the register file and\n  ends at u_if/read_valid_q. A forwarded comparison in EX can change the\n  successor RAM read address, path-valid lookup and collision check on\n  that same edge. The preceding round counted 62,551 EX redirects over\n  ten iterations, so one extra cycle per redirect costs about 1.8% at\n  the champion's cycle count.\nhypothesis: |\n  Put a register between branch resolution and the fetch stage. On each\n  accepted EX instruction, capture the recovery pulse, full next PC and\n  the complete predictor/replay update together. IF consumes this packet\n  on the following cycle. Ordinary predictions still advance every cycle.\n  This takes the forwarded operands and target comparison out of the\n  same clock interval as the successor RAM address and validity lookup.\n\n  On detecting a redirect, immediately squash the younger decode slot.\n  Squash decode again while IF applies the registered redirect, so the\n  intervening fetch cannot issue. A stalled EX instruction emits no event.\n  A redirect, including FENCE.I, takes priority over a held or unavailable\n  fetch. Train ordinary successors and control transfers in accepted EX\n  order with the same one-cycle delay. At 3.611 million marker cycles a\n  median above 52.70 MHz clears the tournament margin. The forecast of 8%\n  corresponds to about 54.4 MHz at that cycle count.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep EX resolution, misalignment handling and RVFI next PC tied to\n      the executing instruction. Expose the accepted misprediction or\n      FENCE.I pulse for the local decode squash. Register its target and\n      every btb_update_t field as one event for IF. Clear event enables\n      on reset, bubbles and stalls so a held EX/MEM slot fires only once.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Route registered recovery and training to IF. Flush ID/EX on both\n      the resolving pulse and the following registered recovery cycle.\n      The older instruction continues to MEM and retires on channel zero.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check an unpredicted transfer with stores on both wrong-path fetch\n      slots, a stalled older load feeding JALR, an aligned changing target,\n      and FENCE.I followed by replacement code. Check exact RVFI PCs,\n      link values, retirement order and bus writes. Check consecutive\n      correctly predicted controls still issue one per ready cycle.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
