{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json,pprint\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(s)\n if 'r7s1' in r['id']: \n  for k in r:\n   print(k, str(r[k])[:7500])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "id hyp-20261121-002-r7s1\ntitle Decouple instruction replay from issue with a stored successor and a one-entry buffer\ncategory structural\nmotivation The champion takes 3,399,350 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Its\nreplay RAM can supply only the first sixteen ordinary instructions after\na recognized control transfer. Earlier PC-indexed replay saved 182,462\ncycles, but in the median placement the path from the target RAM through\nthe hazard unit was within 0.007 ns of the critical path. A fetch engine\nthat can read one instruction ahead during an issue stall can take that\nstall decision off the RAM address path.\n\nhypothesis {'motivation': 'The champion takes 3,399,350 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Its\\nreplay RAM can supply only the first sixteen ordinary instructions after\\na recognized control transfer. Earlier PC-indexed replay saved 182,462\\ncycles, but in the median placement the path from the target RAM through\\nthe hazard unit was within 0.007 ns of the critical path. A fetch engine\\nthat can read one instruction ahead during an issue stall can take that\\nstall decision off the RAM address path.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Give each of 2,048 instruction rows an explicit predicted successor.\\nStore PC+4 for an ordinary instruction or a predicted fall-through, and\\nthe resolved target for a predicted-taken control transfer. On the fast\\npath the registered row supplies the next RAM index directly. Keep the\\narchitectural fetch PC independent and check the returned row against\\nit before using either the instruction or its prediction.\\n\\nWhen decode stalls on a verified row, save the current row in a one-entry\\nbuffer. The RAM can finish the successor read on that same edge and hold\\nits output until decode consumes the buffered instruction. Both words\\nthen issue in order without another lookup. This handles a dependency\\nor a data-bus wait without feeding the hazard signal into the read-address\\nmux. At the issue boundary, a registered valid bit kills wrong-path work\\nwithout routing EX recovery through every \n…[7501 of 10573 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b612ebbc9daa9076]…\nctations for link recovery and retain the exact\\nretirement, trap, load data and wrong-path store assertions.\\n'}]\noutcome improvement\nfitness 188.41\ndelta_pct 10.56\nvs_baseline 1454.54\nfmax_mhz 60.741\nipc_coremark 3e-06\ncycles 3223900\niterations 10\nlut4 3881\nff 1190\nseeds [57.123, 60.741, 60.741]\nperturbations [[0, 57.123, 15], [1, 60.741, 23], [2, 60.741, 23]]\nlutram 8\nbsram 15\ndsp 2\ncritical_path {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.rd_1_s0', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -12.506, 'arrival_ns': 22.801, 'logic_cells': 17, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nlogic_levels 15\nformal_passed True\ncosim_passed True\nerror None\nimplementation_notes \ntimestamp 2026-11-21T16:59:18.599933Z\nslot 1\nround_id 7\nlesson - 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r7s1 (improvement, +10.56%): Buffering the PC-verified instruction on a decode stall let the replay RAM follow its stored successor on the same edge, preserving issue order while keeping the stall signal off the RAM read-address mux.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
