{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_31", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r6s1','r6s0','r7s0','r7s2')): print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r6s0 Split the six EX rounds by unsigned divisor magnitude D. For\n0 < D < 4096 the restored remainder fits in 12 bits, so each round\ncan process five dividend bits with five 13-bit trials. For D >= 4096\nthe top 12 bits of the dividend magnitude A are already below D.\nSeed the remainder with that prefix and consume three bits per round.\nBoth modes then reach the same boundary after bit 2, where the existing\nMEM logic consumes A[1:0]. This puts at most three wide trials in\nseries, with the original issue, hold and retirement timing.\n\nA scratch prototype reached 52.578, 54.540 and 54.540 MHz on Gowin\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2. All three worst paths now start at the\nregister file. With both bus stalls enabled, ten CoreMark iterations\npassed the canonical CRC and UART checks in 3,399,350 marker cycles.\nThe median gives 160.44 iterations/s, 6.09% above the champion.\nOption zero used 3,974 LUT4s and 956 registers. All 55 ALU,\nmultiplier and pipeline cocotb cases passed on this prototype.\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r6s1 Give the divider three independent feedback lanes. A small divisor bounds\nthe remainder width. A large divisor bounds how many quotient digits can\nbe nonzero. Use those facts to process five bits per clock for a 12-bit\ndivisor, three for a divisor of up to 24 bits, and one for larger divisors.\nEach lane consumes its part of the upper 30 dividend bits in six clocks.\nOn the sixth edge, register the selected lane's updated quotient and\nremainder together. MEM can then complete the two low bits and restore the\nsign directly from these registers.\n\nA scratch prototype reached 51.143, 54.790 and 54.790 MHz on Gowin\nplacements 0, 1 and 2. With the lane selection on the input to the MEM\ntail, the same three-lane design reached 53.100 MHz median. Registering\nthe merge on round six lifted the median to 54.790 MHz and moved its\nworst path to fetch replay control. The prototype used 4,054 LUT4s and\n1,109 FFs against the champion's 3,824 and 953. Ten CoreMark iterations\npassed all five CRCs in 3,399,350 mark\n…[2570 of 5642 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c7fbe564aac2e444]…\nx modulo 2,048. Store the remaining\nseven tag bits from the actual destination, including the carry across\nan 8 KiB boundary. The taken bank needs the full destination word tag.\n\nA scratch prototype using 2,048 fall-through rows and 512 taken rows\ncompleted ten iterations in 3,207,645 marker cycles with both bus stall\ngenerators and all five CRCs correct. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2\nreached 54.800, 59.009 and 59.009 MHz. The median yields 183.96 iter/s,\n7.95 percent above the champion. A paired build storing all eleven low\nbits took exactly the same cycles and reached 57.114 MHz median. The\ncompact layout used 17 BSRAMs across the timed design. Placement remains\na risk since the acceptance threshold at this cycle count is 57.176 MHz.\nSelftest and all three stalled random traces matched the reference.\nFive existing pipeline cases stopped at fetch-visit assertions, including\nthe replacement-word collision case. Implementation must verify the\nresulting retirements and newest trained word under those schedules.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
