{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor d in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if d['id'].endswith(('r8s0','r8s1','r8s2','r5s1')):\n  print(d['id'],d['hypothesis'],d['implementation_notes'],d['lesson'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-002-r5s1 {'motivation': 'The champion waits until MEM to issue every load. Even with an idle data\\nbus and a ready base register, an immediately dependent branch or second\\nload incurs a bubble. The ten-iteration instruction trace contains\\n135,320 adjacent load-to-branch register matches and 59,350 adjacent\\nload-to-load matches. Giving a load an earlier bus slot can both deliver\\nits result to the next instruction and absorb a cycle of memory latency.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Schedule the single data port one edge ahead. An older store or an\\noutstanding load gets the registered address for its MEM cycle. When\\nthat slot is free, decode can prepare an effective address for a younger\\nload and register a grant beside ID/EX. In EX, a valid granted load to\\naligned ordinary RAM can complete on the ready handshake. Its immediate\\nconsumer then issues and takes the registered result from EX/MEM.\\nOtherwise the load follows the existing MEM and load-use interlock.\\n\\nA scratch implementation completed ten CoreMark iterations in 3,288,627\\nmarker cycles with both stall generators and all five canonical CRCs.\\nThis saves 259,777 cycles versus the champion. Gowin placements 0, 1\\nand 2 reached 48.382, 49.029 and 49.029 MHz. The median yields 149.09\\niterations/s, 6.86% above 139.52. At this cycle count the acceptance\\nthreshold is 47.994 MHz. Option 0 used 4,295 LUT4s and 1,092 FFs.\\nSelftest and all three random programs matched the reference retirement\\ntraces under both bus stalls.\\n', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 7, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': \"Drive io_dmemAddr directly from one clock-enabled 32-bit address\\nregister so Gowin can infer block RAM for the attached memory. Hold\\nit while an older MEM transaction waits. On advance, first reserve\\nthe next slot for an EX store or an EX load that did not complete.\\nOtherwise prepare the decoding instruction's candidate address.\\nRegister grant eligibility when ID advances and qualify use with\\nID/EX.valid\n…[16252 of 19324 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9d798013e4e97065]…\nr MEM extraction, RVFI, and the existing BEQ/BNE and pointer\\nbypasses. Give ready bus data priority and preserve store ordering,\\naccess masks, misalignment traps and MMIO handshakes.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Warm the predictor and force waits on repeated LW, LH and LBU.\\nCheck return values, sign extension, RVFI address and byte masks,\\nadjacent pointer and branch consumers, and accepted transactions.\\nCross a 64-byte boundary in both directions to force incorrect\\ncarries. Exercise same-index tags 4 KiB apart, a partial store,\\na same-edge fill collision, reset during use and a sustained miss\\nwith changing unaccepted bus data.\\n'}]}  - 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r8s2 (regression, +2.96%): Using displacement bit 5 to predict the carry into address bit 6 let verified loads bypass RAM waits and saved 145,087 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations in the prototype, but registering the corrected low address in EX/MEM still yielded only 193.99 iterations/s against the 197.08 target.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
