{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg --files -g '*coremark*' -g '*trace*' -g 'AGENTS*' && cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "bench/programs/gen_random_trace.py\nbench/programs/coremark/coremark.h\nbench/programs/coremark/coremark.md5\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/coremark_profile_o0_joined.png\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/html/files/coremark-h.html\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r1s0 (regression, +42.82%): Testing 15 divisor multiples in parallel raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 7.774 MHz, but chaining eight radix-16 remainder selections in one EX cycle kept the core below the multicycle divider's 43.9 MHz.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r1s1 (improvement, +705.78%): Registering the shared EX divider after every six restoring steps shortened the critical path, and CoreMark incurred no divide stalls because it executed no DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU between timing markers.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.76%): Predicting branches with 32 two-bit counters and steering JALs in fetch avoided one-cycle EX redirects and cut CoreMark cycles by 6.4%, but the combinational EX divider kept Fmax near 5.5 MHz versus 43.9 MHz with multicycle division.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r2s0 (regression, +8.63%): Testing D, 2D and 3D in parallel cut each six-bit divider round from six dependent remainder selections to three, raising Fmax until a path from the register file set the limit.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r2s1 (regression, +5.00%): Splitting at divisor magnitude 2^14 raised Fmax at the same six-round latency because small divisors need only 15-bit trial subtractors and seeding the wide remainder with the top 14 dividend bits cuts the 33-bit subtractor chain from six to three per round.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r2s2 (improvement, +9.91%): Storing the control-transfer instruction word in the 64-entry BTB let a tag hit satisfy fetch during imem stalls even when the predictor chose fall-through, saving 154,266 CoreMark cycles over the same BTB with replay disabled.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r3s1 (regression, +12.14%): Replaying ordinary instructions from a 256-word backedge region during imem stalls saved 504,231 CoreMark cycles\n…[4772 of 7844 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:df9cb34ec2695904]…\n Masking wrong-path replay hits with `recovering` took the load-dependent redirect off the replay resets but left the ALU-to-RAM address path critical at 71.917 MHz median, while parallel address sums, byte-lane load comparisons and reuse of the divider’s final lane registers raised the median to 76.247 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r9s1 (improvement, +9.54%): Registering per-source bypass enables during producer EX removed `EX/MEM.mem_read` from the next address calculation, and comparing active RAM bytes in parallel with an extension check on the other operand let dependent BEQ/BNE resolve without waiting for load formatting.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r9s2 (regression, +5.88%): Looking up load words during operand collection let adjacent branches and pointer loads use a registered operand in EX while MEM verified the hit, but the prototype’s 4.1% reduction in CoreMark cycles could not offset its lower median Fmax relative to the round winner (72.784 vs 78.330 MHz).\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
