{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads, open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\")):\n if r['category']=='predictor' and r['round_id'] in (4,5,6,7,8) or r[\\\"id\\\"]==\\\"hyp-20261121-002-r5s1\\\":\n  print(r['id'],r['cycles'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'] if isinstance(r['hypothesis'],dict) else r['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r4s2 3359136 Use a 4,096-entry, direct-mapped table of instruction words and next-PC\npredictions. Present the selected next PC to synchronous RAM on the same\nedge that installs it in the PC register. Each tagged word can serve an\nimem wait regardless of which branch led to it. Conditional branches in\nthe same aligned 16 KiB region get independent targets and two-bit\ndirection counters. Jumps predict their last resolved aligned target.\n\nA scratch RTL prototype cut the unavailable-fetch count to 439 and EX\nredirects from 62,551 to 41,173. Ten iterations took 3,359,136 marker\ncycles with both stall generators enabled and all five CRCs matching.\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 43.307, 43.269 and 43.269 MHz.\nThe median gives 128.81 iterations/s, 5.26% above the champion. The\nacceptance threshold at this cycle count is about 42.997 MHz. Option\nzero used 3,595 LUT4s and 23 BSRAMs. The prototype passed all 53\nsingle-retirement formal checks and matched the reference on selftest\nand all three random programs with both stall generators enabled.\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r5s1 3288627 Schedule the single data port one edge ahead. An older store or an\noutstanding load gets the registered address for its MEM cycle. When\nthat slot is free, decode can prepare an effective address for a younger\nload and register a grant beside ID/EX. In EX, a valid granted load to\naligned ordinary RAM can complete on the ready handshake. Its immediate\nconsumer then issues and takes the registered result from EX/MEM.\nOtherwise the load follows the existing MEM and load-use interlock.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten CoreMark iterations in 3,288,627\nmarker cycles with both stall generators and all five canonical CRCs.\nThis saves 259,777 cycles versus the champion. Gowin placements 0, 1\nand 2 reached 48.382, 49.029 and 49.029 MHz. The median yields 149.09\niterations/s, 6.86% above 139.52. At this cycle count the acceptance\nthreshold is 47.994 MHz. Option 0 used 4,295 LUT4s and 1,092 FFs.\nSelftest and all three random progr\n…[5587 of 8659 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2fe3654c1193099f]…\nEX/MEM register. In MEM, qualify the word with its valid bit, full address,\nordinary-RAM range, natural alignment and write-collision status. An\naccepted bus read supplies the live word. During backpressure a\nverified prediction can supply the same word and complete the load.\nA wrong carry or tag costs the usual memory wait. Full-word stores\ninstall their data and partial stores invalidate the indexed word.\n\nA scratch RTL prototype took 3,078,813 marker cycles versus 3,223,900\nfor the champion with both stall generators, all five canonical CRCs\nand the validation banner. This saves 145,087 cycles. It needs median\nGowin Fmax above 60.6763 MHz to clear 197.07686 iterations/s. That\nnarrow timing margin is the main risk. The version using the general\nALU result for the external address reached 59.588 MHz median and\n193.54 iterations/s. Its worst path on placement 1 ran from a WB\nregister identifier through EX forwarding and the ALU to data RAM.\nRegistering the corrected low address separately targets that path.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
