{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_68","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat > \"$TMPDIR/probe/p12.py\" <<'\"'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport os\nd=Path(os.environ['TMPDIR'])/'probe';D=d/'v12';D.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nfor f in (d/'v11').glob('*.sv'): (D/f.name).write_text(f.read_text())\np=D/'load_predictor.sv';s=p.read_text().replace('logic [32:0] bank','logic [31:0] bank').replace('logic [32:0] early_row, late_row;', 'logic [31:0] early_row, late_row; logic early_valid;')\ns=s.replace('      if (set_valid) bank[w_ix][32] <= w_row[32];\\\\n','')\ns=s.replace('      early_ix <= early_addr[11:2];','      early_valid <= lane_mask[early_addr[11:5]][early_addr[4:2]];\\\\n      early_ix <= early_addr[11:2];').replace('early_row[32]', 'early_valid')\np.write_text(s)\n# Keep verification and label/counter fanout off the early RAM feedback loop.\np=D/'core.sv';s=p.read_text()\ns=s.replace(\\\"assign early_addr[11:6] = early_base[11:6] + waiting.imm[11:6] + {5'd0,waiting.imm[5]};\\\",\\\"assign early_addr[11:6] = early_base[11:6];\\\")\np.write_text(s)\nPY\npython \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/probe/p12.py\"\nPYTHONPATH=. TEST_RTL=v12 python \"$TMPDIR/probe/time.py\" t12 0 1 2 > \"$TMPDIR/probe/t12.log\" 2>&1'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_76","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'NRET=1 RTL_DIR=\"$TMPDIR/probe/v14\" OBJ_DIR=\"$TMPDIR/probe/obj\" bash test/cosim/build.sh > \"$TMPDIR/probe/b14.log\" 2>&1 && \"$TMPDIR/probe/obj/cosim_sim\" \"$TMPDIR/probe/coremark.elf\" 7500000 --bench --istall --dstall > \"$TMPDIR/probe/s14.log\" && python \"$TMPDIR/probe/check.py\"'","aggregated_output":"selftest [] {'passed': True, 'retired': 115}\nselftest ['--istall', '--dstall'] {'passed': True, 'retired': 115}\nrandom1 [] {'passed': True, 'retired': 11037}\nrandom1 ['--istall', '--dstall'] {'passed': True, 'retired': 11037}\nrandom2 [] {'passed': True, 'retired': 11094}\nrandom2 ['--istall', '--dstall'] {'passed': True, 'retired': 11094}\nrandom3 [] {'passed': True, 'retired': 12059}\nrandom3 ['--istall', '--dstall'] {'passed': True, 'retired': 12059}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_78", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r11s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261121-003-r11s2\ntitle: Predict the load word in collection and retry with the forwarded base\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Ten CoreMark iterations take the champion 3,238,429 marker cycles. Earlier\n  traces counted 544,852 loads and placed 678,641 of 684,483 data references\n  on one 4 KiB page. A lookup started in EX can hide a RAM wait, but its\n  output arrives late for an adjacent consumer. A lookup in collection can\n  supply that consumer on time, but a pointer producer can change the base\n  between collection and EX.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give each load two reads of a coherent resident-page word RAM. As the\n  load leaves collection, predict the word index from the current base and\n  displacement. For a pointer chain, use the preceding load's unverified\n  word as the next base guess. In EX format the predicted word using the\n  real byte offset and capture it in the ordinary EX/MEM forwarding register.\n  In parallel, check the actual word index, RAM page, initialization and\n  freshness. The next instruction may execute from the registered candidate\n  while MEM qualifies it. A verified hit removes both the bus wait and the\n  adjacent load-use bubble.\n  \n  Start the second read in EX at the exact forwarded address. It rescues\n  an incorrect early guess without issuing an external read. Capture late\n  eligibility in EX using an initialized-sector bit. Require every word in\n  the sector to be known before enabling this shortcut. Independently\n  initialized words can still satisfy the early probe. If an adjacent\n  consumer used an incorrect early prediction, annul its effects, hold\n  it in EX, and capture the verified late value or the accepted bus value\n  into each matching operand. Re-execute it once. Independent consumers\n  continue as soon as the load completes.\n  \n  In a scratch implementation this took 3,045,213 marker cycles with both\n  stall generators, saving 193,216 cycles (5.97%) and matching all five\n  CRCs and the validation banner. That cycle count needs a median above\n  82.07 MHz to exceed 269.50 iterations/s. The initial three-placement\n  median was 78.274 MHz. Its worst path ran from a forwarded operand to\n  front-end flush. Shorten verification of a predicted JALR consuming that\n  operand by comparing its base against the expected base computed in\n  collection. This keeps the full target adder off the flush decision.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/load_predictor.sv\n    description: |\n      Use two synchronous read ports on 1,024 coherent raw words. Learn\n      the page tag from ordinary RAM accesses. Replace it after 32 consecutive\n      references to the same competing page. Disable hits and clear validity\n      on reset or page replacement. Record per-word initialization and an\n      all-initialized bit per eight-word sector. Register writes, including\n      accepted bus reads and logically completed stores. A full word sets\n      validity. Partial stores update enabled bytes and preserve prior\n      validity. Reject ambiguous same-index writes on each edge from lookup\n      to use, including a logically completed older store. Keep snapshots\n      associated with their instruction through stalls and page changes.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Predict the early probe base from the raw candidate of an adjacent\n      pointer producer, an older registered result or the waiting operand.\n      Compute word-index bits 5 through 2 and 11 through 6 independently,\n      using displacement bit 5 as the carry guess for the upper slice.\n      Verify the complete word address in EX. Route a verified early hit\n      through the registered bypass. Interlock a consumer on a failed\n      prediction until the real value is captured. Keep x0 and a faulting\n      producer from superseding an older writer. For JALR, register the two\n      possible expected upper base values alongside the load-dependent\n      operand. Register direct-target agreement for PC-relative transfers.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Format the speculative word using the actual lane, width and signedness.\n      Include it in the registered result with a dedicated load enable.\n      Give low MUL a final result select to keep load formatting off its\n      DSP output path. Launch the exact retry read in parallel. Gate all\n      redirects, divide starts, training and EX/MEM effects on operand\n      validation or completed repair. For target verification, compare the\n      upper 31 bits of forwarded rs1 with predicted_target[31:1] minus\n      imm[31:1], subtracting one more when rs1[0] and imm[0] are both set.\n      Use the full architectural sum for recovery, alignment and RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Complete a naturally aligned RAM load from a verified early word,\n      verified retry or accepted external read, in that order. Use the same\n      raw word for formatting and RVFI. Present external requests from a\n      registered address. An ordinary store can update the word RAM and\n      occupy one ordered write-through slot. Hold address, mask and data\n      until acceptance, allow drain and refill, and serialize external\n      reads, MMIO, fences and traps behind the slot. Keep RVFI address\n      and masks tied to the retiring memory operation.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a page, force both successful predictions and wrong pointer or\n      carry guesses, and compare exact retirement and bus records. Exercise\n      matching uses on both source ports, a repaired store, divide and JALR,\n      partial stores to initialized and cold words, a colliding fill, page\n      replacement, a prolonged demand miss, reset, and a marker following\n      a posted store. Check the single replay cycle on a cold adjacent\n      miss and back-to-back retirement on a verified early hit.\n'''.replace('  \\\\n','\\\\n'))\nPY\npython - <<'PY'\nprint(257.65*1.046,3.045213*257.65*1.046/10)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
