{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_75", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r12s2.yaml\ndate -u'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-003-r12s2\ntitle: Predict and refine the divide quotient with one reciprocal table\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion retires 2,922,016 instructions in 3,044,309 CoreMark\n  marker cycles. At one retirement per cycle, eliminating every bubble\n  would gain only 4.19% at the current clock. The divider limits Fmax\n  even though the trace contains zero divide or remainder instructions.\n  Gowin's option-0 worst path crosses 59 cells in the 18-bit divider\n  lane. The median placement also has a critical path in the small\n  divisor lane. Predicting the quotient for every divisor magnitude\n  can remove both restoring chains.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the five restoring lanes with one quotient predictor and an\n  exact residual check. Work on unsigned magnitudes A and D with\n  H = 2^32. For nonzero D set s = max(0, floor(log2(D))-11) and\n  i = D >> s. A synchronous 4,096-row ROM contains\n  min(H-1, floor(H/U)), where U = i for 1 <= i < 2048 and U = i+1\n  for i >= 2048. Give row zero a defined value. Shift the ROM word\n  right by s to obtain M. Rounding the divisor up and its reciprocal\n  down guarantees each predicted quotient contribution is conservative.\n\n  Start with R=A. Twice compute E=high32(R*M), accumulate E into the\n  quotient, and replace R with R-E*D. For A=0xffffffff and D=2049,\n  the first contribution is 2,095,104. The residual supplies another\n  1,023 and leaves R=3072. The final increment gives 2,096,128 with\n  remainder 1023.\n  On round seven compare R with D, increment the quotient and subtract\n  D if needed, then apply the saved architectural sign and zero rules.\n  The low 32 product bits suffice for the residual because E*D <= R.\n\n  The error bound is checkable with integer arithmetic. Let\n  e = 1-D*M/H. Each pass leaves a residual below D+e*R. For D<H/2,\n  the maximum of e+H*e^2/D is below 0.668 over the ROM buckets.\n  Within each bucket M is constant and that expression decreases with\n  D. Checking its smallest divisor covers all 43,007 nonempty buckets\n  below H/2. This giv\n…[1311 of 4383 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:08a690a6d6928623]…\nn rounds four and six use a second multiply-add\n      path to compute the low word of R+E*(-D) from a registered -D.\n      Save the first E on round four and sum both estimates on round\n      six. Register the corrected quotient and remainder on div_last.\n      Keep these arithmetic paths local to the divider so ordinary MUL\n      retains its EX/MEM bypass. Preserve ALTOPS, the seven-round\n      handshake, backpressure, consecutive divides and reset behavior.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check the error inequality at each nonempty bucket's minimum D\n      below H/2 using g=H-D*M and g*D+g*g < H*D. Exercise actual RTL\n      at bucket edges, with maximum dividends, exact multiples and their\n      neighbors. Include a first estimate that misses by hundreds and\n      cases that use the final increment. Compare all four DIV/REM\n      operations with the integer reference, including divide by zero,\n      signed overflow and a result held across downstream stalls.\nSat Nov 21 21:19:21 UTC 2026\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
