{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_93", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,os\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import parse_reports\nt=Path(os.environ['TMPDIR'])\nfor f in ['bypass1tim0','bpt1','bypass1tim2','shadowtim1','alignmasktim1','tagpredtim1','write_onlytim1','trap_recovertim1']:\n p=t/f;m=parse_reports(p);print(f,m.get('fmax_mhz'),m.get('logic'),m.get('regs'),m.get('bsram'))\nPY\ncat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r13s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "bypass1tim0 None None None None\nbpt1 97.26 4883 1934 25\nbypass1tim2 97.26 4883 1934 25\nshadowtim1 None None None None\nalignmasktim1 None None None None\ntagpredtim1 None None None None\nwrite_onlytim1 None None None None\ntrap_recovertim1 None None None None\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r13s2\ntitle: \"Predict aligned accesses when selecting the adjacent register writer\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion already retires 2,922,016 instructions in 3,044,309\n  CoreMark marker cycles. At single retirement width, perfect issue\n  would gain only 4.19% at the current clock. A reproduction of the\n  92.229 MHz Gowin placement shows a different opportunity. Its four\n  longest paths start at bit 0 of EX/MEM.alu_result, pass through the\n  forwarded base, address alignment and ex_writer, and end at the next\n  instruction's saved operand. Every adjacent dependency pays for a\n  fault check even when the producer is an ordinary ADD.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict that a valid, decoded register writer will produce its rd.\n  Drive bypass_writer from in.valid && in.ctrl.reg_write, ahead of\n  link_fault and data_fault. The existing nonzero-rd comparisons in\n  collection can then choose the adjacent bypass and mask older sources\n  while EX computes the low bits of the forwarded address.\n\n  Verify alignment in parallel using the existing architectural checks.\n  On an advancing instruction with link_fault or data_fault, force the\n  existing EX redirect to sequential_pc, even if fetch already predicted\n  fall-through. This flushes the younger operand selected under the\n  incorrect writer prediction. Its refetched copy sees the youngest\n  surviving value of rd through normal register read and forwarding.\n  A misaligned store can take the same recovery path. The faulting\n  instruction advances once with its original trap and suppressed side\n  effects. Keep ctrl_with_trap.reg_write on the MEM and architectural\n  write paths. The existing advancing gate prevents a held or\n  load-repairing instruction from initiating recovery.\n\n  A scratch prototype made those two EX control changes. It completed\n  ten iterations in 3,044,309 marker cycles with both stall generators,\n  all five canonical CRCs and the validation banner. Gowin placements 1\n  and 2 both rose from 92.229 to 97.260 MHz. At that median the prototype\n  delivers 319.48 iterations/s, 5.45% above the champion. Selftest and\n  all three random programs matched the ISS with both buses stalled.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Set bypass_writer to in.valid && in.ctrl.reg_write. Set redirect\n      to advancing && (redirect_always || bad_jalr || link_fault ||\n      data_fault || (branch_cond ? redirect_on_taken : redirect_on_fallthrough)).\n      Both fault cases already select sequential_pc in redirect_target.\n      Retain fault-qualified write enables, load/store masks, trap state,\n      load repair, backpressure, and all existing branch and FENCE.I\n      recovery conditions.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      In misaligned_load_preserves_older_writer require three clocks from\n      the faulting LW to its dependent ADD. In adjacent_faults_with_forwarded_addresses\n      require gaps of 2, 3 and 3 at PCs 12, 28 and 44. The aligned load\n      still takes its one repair cycle. Keep the checks for both operands,\n      the surviving older writer, trap, PC order and bus effects.\n      Add a cold LW held for five data waits ahead of a faulting LH to x5,\n      followed by SW using x5. Require one accepted read, one trap, and\n      exactly one accepted store containing the pre-fault x5 value.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
