{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_109", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r14s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r14s2\ntitle: Predict a counted loop's exit when writing its next replay successor\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Between the timing markers the champion resolves 529,588 conditional\n  branches and redirects on 43,919 of them. Two branches each produce\n  3,241 redirects in 29,160 visits. Each repeats eight taken outcomes\n  followed by one exit. A two-bit counter quickly learns the backedge\n  but misses nearly every exit. The replay RAM already has a place to\n  store the predicted successor for a branch's next visit.\nhypothesis: |\n  Learn how many consecutive continuation outcomes precede an exit.\n  Use taken as the continuation for a negative displacement and\n  fall-through for a positive displacement. For each of 256 PC-indexed\n  histories store a six-bit saturating run count, the last completed run\n  length and one confidence bit. An exit establishes confidence when\n  two consecutive nonzero, unsaturated run lengths match. A changed\n  length, an overrun or saturation clears confidence. The existing\n  two-bit prediction supplies the direction while confidence is clear.\n\n  Read this small table in decode. During operand collection prepare\n  the next history and direction for both possible outcomes. When a\n  legal, cacheable conditional branch advances in EX, its actual\n  direction selects exactly one training transaction. After the final\n  expected continuation, write fall-through for a backward branch or\n  the target for a forward branch into the normal replay row. After\n  the observed exit, write the continuation for the next invocation.\n  Encode an override as a weak counter in the chosen direction. On\n  every other fill use the existing counter. Fetch can follow the\n  stored link at the existing latency.\n\n  A scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,011,033 marker\n  cycles with both stall generators, all five canonical CRCs and the\n  validation banner. That saves 33,276 cycles over the champion.\n  Putting the history in the wide fetch row saved only another 1,117\n  cycles and reached 91.004 MHz on Gowin placement 1. Keep the history\n  on its own training port. With 3,011,033 cycles, promotion requires\n  a median above 100.622 MHz. Timing closure is the main uncertainty.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Read a 256 by 13 history RAM with fetch PC bits 9 through 2 on\n      decode acceptance. Register both candidate updates as collection\n      advances. Hold them through memory and operand repair stalls.\n      Pipeline the history write from the advancing EX branch. Reset\n      the initialized bits on reset or FENCE.I and treat same-index\n      read/write collisions as cold. Index aliases may change a\n      prediction and are always checked by normal EX resolution.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Extend the training transaction with actual outcome and the\n      candidate loop-override bits. The lookup and prediction bundles\n      delivered to decode keep their current widths.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export the resolved branch_taken with the existing advancing,\n      legal, aligned, in-RAM update. Use the fully forwarded operands\n      and the existing mismatch, trap and redirect decisions.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Register the override information with the existing EX fill.\n      Use the selected counter MSB to prepare both the row counter\n      and its successor so they always describe the same prediction.\n      Ordinary rows, jumps, replay qualification and FENCE.I use the\n      existing fill and lookup rules.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Train a repeated eight-continuation loop, then change its trip\n      count. Check that the warm final continuation predicts the exit,\n      that a wrong count recovers to the architectural PC, and that\n      each store commits once. Cover the forward-exit form, a saturated\n      count and a held branch with an older stalled load.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema,pathlib\np=pathlib.Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r14s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']==p.stem and all((p.parents[2]/i['file']).is_file() for i in d['changes'])\nprint('valid',p)\nPY\nPYTHONPATH=. python \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/predictstudy/summary.py\" word256 word512 word1024 local64 local128 local256 threeloop rowsteer allsteer fill256 fill64\ntail -4 \"$TMPDIR/predictstudy/fill256/pipeline.log\"\ndate -u'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_109", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r14s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r14s2\ntitle: Predict a counted loop's exit when writing its next replay successor\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Between the timing markers the champion resolves 529,588 conditional\n  branches and redirects on 43,919 of them. Two branches each produce\n  3,241 redirects in 29,160 visits. Each repeats eight taken outcomes\n  followed by one exit. A two-bit counter quickly learns the backedge\n  but misses nearly every exit. The replay RAM already has a place to\n  store the predicted successor for a branch's next visit.\nhypothesis: |\n  Learn how many consecutive continuation outcomes precede an exit.\n  Use taken as the continuation for a negative displacement and\n  fall-through for a positive displacement. For each of 256 PC-indexed\n  histories store a six-bit saturating run count, the last completed run\n  length and one confidence bit. An exit establishes confidence when\n  two consecutive nonzero, unsaturated run lengths match. A changed\n  length, an overrun or saturation clears confidence. The existing\n  two-bit prediction supplies the direction while confidence is clear.\n\n  Read this small table in decode. During operand collection prepare\n  the next history and direction for both possible outcomes. When a\n  legal, cacheable conditional branch advances in EX, its actual\n  direction selects exactly one training transaction. After the final\n  expected continuation, write fall-through for a backward branch or\n  the target for a forward branch into the normal replay row. After\n  the observed exit, write the continuation for the next invocation.\n  Encode an override as a weak counter in the chosen direction. On\n  every other fill use the existing counter. Fetch can follow the\n  stored link at the existing latency.\n\n  A scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,011,033 marker\n  cycles with both stall generators, all five canonical CRCs and the\n  validation banner. That saves 33,276 cycles over the champion.\n  Putting the history in the wide fetch row saved only another 1,117\n  cycles and reached 91.004 MHz on Gowin placement 1. Keep the history\n  on its own training port. With 3,011,033 cycles, promotion requires\n  a median above 100.622 MHz. Timing closure is the main uncertainty.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Read a 256 by 13 history RAM with fetch PC bits 9 through 2 on\n      decode acceptance. Register both candidate updates as collection\n      advances. Hold them through memory and operand repair stalls.\n      Pipeline the history write from the advancing EX branch. Reset\n      the initialized bits on reset or FENCE.I and treat same-index\n      read/write collisions as cold. Index aliases may change a\n      prediction and are always checked by normal EX resolution.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Extend the training transaction with actual outcome and the\n      candidate loop-override bits. The lookup and prediction bundles\n      delivered to decode keep their current widths.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export the resolved branch_taken with the existing advancing,\n      legal, aligned, in-RAM update. Use the fully forwarded operands\n      and the existing mismatch, trap and redirect decisions.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Register the override information with the existing EX fill.\n      Use the selected counter MSB to prepare both the row counter\n      and its successor so they always describe the same prediction.\n      Ordinary rows, jumps, replay qualification and FENCE.I use the\n      existing fill and lookup rules.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Train a repeated eight-continuation loop, then change its trip\n      count. Check that the warm final continuation predicts the exit,\n      that a wrong count recovers to the architectural PC, and that\n      each store commits once. Cover the forward-exit form, a saturated\n      count and a held branch with an older stalled load.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema,pathlib\np=pathlib.Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r14s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']==p.stem and all((p.parents[2]/i['file']).is_file() for i in d['changes'])\nprint('valid',p)\nPY\nPYTHONPATH=. python \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/predictstudy/summary.py\" word256 word512 word1024 local64 local128 local256 threeloop rowsteer allsteer fill256 fill64\ntail -4 \"$TMPDIR/predictstudy/fill256/pipeline.log\"\ndate -u'", "aggregated_output": "valid cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r14s2.yaml\nword256 None 3048131 True 0.0 None\nword512 77.542 3046988 True 254.49 cpu/u_predict/tags_tags_0_0_s0\nword1024 None 3043079 True 0.0 None\nlocal64 None 3030814 True 0.0 None\nlocal128 None 3030440 True 0.0 None\nlocal256 None 3011033 True 0.0 None\nthreeloop None 3009916 True 0.0 None\nrowsteer None 3009916 True 0.0 None\nallsteer None 3009916 True 0.0 None\nfill256 None 3011033 True 0.0 None\nfill64 None 3032398 True 0.0 None\n-- Docs: https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/capture-warnings.html\n=========================== short test summary info ============================\nERROR ../../../../../../tmp - PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied: ...\n2 warnings, 1 error in 0.06s\nSat Nov 21 23:18:00 UTC 2026\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
