{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short --untracked-files=all && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r1s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261121-003-r1s2\ntitle: Predict direct control flow with 32 two-bit counters in fetch\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Fetch currently advances to PC+4 on every accepted instruction. IF/ID is\n  combinational, so each aligned taken branch or jump resolved in EX costs\n  one fetch opportunity. A replay of the canonical CoreMark instruction trace\n  reproduced the baseline's 4,491,485 marker cycles with the harness stall\n  sequence. It counted 294,425 taken conditional branches and 49,367 JALs.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use 32 two-bit saturating counters indexed by fetch PC[6:2]. Read the\n  counter in parallel with instruction memory. For a legal conditional branch\n  choose its PC-relative target when the high counter bit is set. For JAL\n  always choose its PC-relative target. Use PC+4 for other opcodes, including\n  JALR, and for a speculative target outside aligned instruction RAM.\n  Compute the target from the fetched instruction's B/J displacement.\n  Carry the selected direction and counter snapshot with the accepted\n  instruction into EX. Because the direct target is determined by those\n  same instruction bits, EX can detect a direction error with one bit.\n  Train once per valid, advancing legal conditional branch using its actual\n  outcome. Correct to the resolved next PC on a direction error or an aligned\n  JALR. Keep trap, link and RVFI next-PC decisions under EX control.\n  A temporary RTL prototype completed the ten-iteration CoreMark run in\n  4,203,476 marker cycles with all five expected CRCs. Gowin placement options\n  0, 1 and 2 yielded 5.165, 5.484 and 5.484 MHz. The median gives 13.05\n  iterations/s, 7.64% above the 12.12 baseline. At this cycle count the\n  implementation needs a median above 5.329 MHz to clear the acceptance\n  threshold. The prototype used 12,670 LUT4s against the baseline's 12,312.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Put the counter table and direct-target adder beside the PC register.\n      Start counters weakly not taken. Decode raw imem_data, including legal\n      branch funct3 and target alignment, independently of the flush mux.\n      Apply a prediction only on fetch acceptance. An EX correction takes PC\n      priority over instruction-bus backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry predicted_taken and the two-bit counter snapshot in if_id_t and id_ex_t.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Capture prediction metadata with its instruction, hold it across data stalls and clear it on a bubble or flush.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Compare predicted_taken with the actual aligned transfer on a valid EX\n      advance. Feed the existing hazard redirect input only on recovery,\n      selecting PC+4 if predicted taken, or the resolved target otherwise.\n      This lets target selection run in parallel with operand comparison.\n      Export the EX branch index, saturating increment or decrement of its\n      counter snapshot, and write enable on that same advance.\n      Preserve misalignment traps, forwarded branch operands and JAL/JALR link data.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Route EX training to IF and use the correction signal for the existing PC redirect and younger-instruction flush controls.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise a warming backward loop and its exit, forward taken branches,\n      JAL, JALR and misaligned targets. Check the RVFI PC sequence and absence\n      of wrong-path stores with an imem stall at recovery and an older dmem\n      operation holding the resolving branch in EX.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
