{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_89", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r2s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-003-r2s2\ntitle: Serve recurring control transfers from a 64-entry branch target buffer\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion spends 4,491,485 marker cycles per ten CoreMark iterations.\n  An aligned taken transfer discards one fetch opportunity when EX resolves it,\n  and a recurring branch still waits for imem even after its direction is\n  predictable. The previous 32-counter predictor saved 6.4% of cycles on\n  the original core. On the champion, a scratch implementation of that design\n  ran in 4,203,401 cycles at 41.304 MHz, below the tournament cutoff.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give each entry of a 64-entry, direct-mapped branch target buffer (BTB)\n  its source instruction word as well as a tag, resolved target and two-bit\n  counter. A tag hit can supply both the control transfer during an imem wait\n  and its predicted successor. This exploits repeated visits to a branch\n  even when its counter predicts fall-through. Only resolved conditional\n  branches, JALs and JALRs can populate the buffer.\n\n  With instruction replay disabled, the prototype took 4,197,517 marker\n  cycles. Serving the saved branch word on a hit reduced that to 4,043,251\n  with all five expected CRCs. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached\n  43.124, 43.014 and 43.014 MHz. The median gives 106.38 iterations/s,\n  8.9% above the champion. The design needs 41.303 MHz at this cycle count\n  to clear the 4.6% cutoff. Placement option 0 used 3,015 LUT4s and 8 BSRAMs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Index by PC[7:2]. Match a valid entry's PC[19:8] tag and require\n      PC[31:20] to be zero. Store the aligned target's bits [19:2],\n      the original 32-bit instruction and a two-bit counter in parallel\n      arrays with one write port. Reset the 64 valid bits. On a hit,\n      select external data whenever imem is ready and use the saved word\n      when imem stalls. Predict the stored target when the counter MSB\n      is set and the selected instr\n…[1500 of 4572 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b02ad16b5d6426dc]…\nALR target.\n      On an advancing FENCE.I, invalidate the BTB, squash the younger fetch\n      and restart at PC+4 so modified code is fetched again.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Route imem readiness and BTB feedback. Use imemReady OR tag hit as fetch availability for the hazard unit. Feed the selected instruction to decode and load-use detection.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Emit an IF bubble on unavailable fetch data. Let EX recovery clear\n      ID/EX directly and override the fetch hold. Preserve the existing\n      priority of older dmem stalls and divide waits.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a loop, stall imem on a BTB hit and an index collision, and\n      check both fetch acceptance and RVFI PC pairs. Alternate targets at\n      one JALR and place a store on the wrong path. Exercise recovery with\n      an older stalled load, a misaligned target, and replacement code\n      after FENCE.I. Check store strobes, link values and retirement order.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
