{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n a=json.loads(l)\n if a.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(a.keys()); print(a['hypothesis']); print(a.get('implementation_notes')); print(a.get('metrics'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "dict_keys(['id', 'title', 'category', 'motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes', 'outcome', 'fitness', 'delta_pct', 'vs_baseline', 'fmax_mhz', 'ipc_coremark', 'cycles', 'iterations', 'lut4', 'ff', 'seeds', 'perturbations', 'lutram', 'bsram', 'dsp', 'critical_path', 'logic_levels', 'formal_passed', 'cosim_passed', 'error', 'implementation_notes', 'timestamp', 'slot', 'round_id', 'lesson'])\n{'motivation': \"Fetch currently advances to PC+4 on every accepted instruction. IF/ID is\\ncombinational, so each aligned taken branch or jump resolved in EX costs\\none fetch opportunity. A replay of the canonical CoreMark instruction trace\\nreproduced the baseline's 4,491,485 marker cycles with the harness stall\\nsequence. It counted 294,425 taken conditional branches and 49,367 JALs.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Use 32 two-bit saturating counters indexed by fetch PC[6:2]. Read the\\ncounter in parallel with instruction memory. For a legal conditional branch\\nchoose its PC-relative target when the high counter bit is set. For JAL\\nalways choose its PC-relative target. Use PC+4 for other opcodes, including\\nJALR, and for a speculative target outside aligned instruction RAM.\\nCompute the target from the fetched instruction's B/J displacement.\\nCarry the selected direction and counter snapshot with the accepted\\ninstruction into EX. Because the direct target is determined by those\\nsame instruction bits, EX can detect a direction error with one bit.\\nTrain once per valid, advancing legal conditional branch using its actual\\noutcome. Correct to the resolved next PC on a direction error or an aligned\\nJALR. Keep trap, link and RVFI next-PC decisions under EX control.\\nA temporary RTL prototype completed the ten-iteration CoreMark run in\\n4,203,476 marker cycles with all five expected CRCs. Gowin placement options\\n0, 1 and 2 yielded 5.165, 5.484 and 5.484 MHz. The median gives 13.05\\niterations/s, 7.64% above the 12.12 baseline. At this cycle count the\\nimplementation needs a median above 5.329 MHz to clear the acceptance\\nthresh\n…[10458 of 13530 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6bd045ca6624f44f]…\n invalidate\\nfields on the existing EX-to-IF connection.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Export advancing and the resolved aligned transfer direction. Use the\\nexisting BTB write qualification to identify an anchor. Allow a successor\\npayload write for a decoded legal non-branch, non-jump instruction at an\\naligned RAM PC on EX advance. FENCE.I invalidation overrides the write.\\nKeep recovery and RVFI next PC driven by the full architectural resolution.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Warm a loop and withhold imem throughout its body, including across a\\nload-use interlock and a four-cycle dmem hold. Check that a 17-word body\\ncan replay the first 16 and must wait for word 17. Change branch direction,\\nalias two source branches to one index and retarget a JALR with a store\\non the old path. Check the RVFI PC pairs and store strobes. Execute\\nFENCE.I, revisit the same branch and require its first successor to wait\\nfor the replacement instruction on the bus.\\n'}]}\n\nNone\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
