{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_106", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -5 \"$TMPDIR/research/formalplain.log\"; sed -n '\"'1,80p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r4s2.yaml; date -u\"", "aggregated_output": "SBY 15:39:06 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\nSBY 15:39:06 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\nSBY 15:39:06 [reg_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\n\nFormal: 53 passed, 0 failed\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r4s2\ntitle: Predict and replay the next fetch from a PC-indexed instruction table\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion took 3,511,720 marker cycles. Its 64 branch indices each own\n  two 16-word successor sequences. A branch collision can lose both the\n  prediction and access to a trained sequence. Long straight-line runs\n  exhaust the sequence, and converging paths store copies of the same words.\n  A champion run with only dmem stalls took 3,379,672 cycles. Closing that\n  fetch gap alone would gain about 3.9% at equal Fmax. Clearing 4.6% also\n  calls for fewer control redirects or a faster clock.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use a 4,096-entry, direct-mapped table of instruction words and next-PC\n  predictions. Look up the predicted next word PC on the edge that advances\n  fetch. Each entry identifies its own source PC, supplies its instruction\n  during an imem wait, and predicts either fall-through or a saved aligned\n  control target. This lets every incoming edge reuse the same training.\n  It also gives distinct control PCs within an aligned 16 KiB region independent\n  targets and direction counters. Train from advancing EX instructions.\n  A scratch RTL prototype ran ten iterations in 3,359,136 marker cycles\n  with all five CRCs. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 43.307,\n  43.269 and 43.269 MHz. The median gives 128.81 iterations/s, 5.26%\n  above the champion. At this cycle count the 4.6% cutoff needs more than\n  42.997 MHz. Placement option zero used 3,595 LUT4s and 23 BSRAMs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the branch-indexed paths and their cursors with one synchronous\n      lookup per fetch. Index by PC[13:2], store PC[19:14] as a tag, and check\n      the full aligned RAM range. Read at the recovery PC, accepted predicted\n      PC, or held PC in that priority order. Store the word, control flag,\n      counter and aligned target as a 59-bit vector. Describe a flat word\n      array with a synchronous write and synchronous read into a packed\n      register so Gowin infers block RAM. Use the existing update.write\n      and update.successor enables. prediction.hit means a matching\n      control entry for EX counter training. An ordinary hit supplies its\n      instruction and selects PC+4. Use 256 resettable group-valid bits and\n      a table of 16-bit initialized-word masks. The first write to an\n      invalid group starts a fresh mask. Qualify the registered mask with\n      group validity and forward the new mask on a same-group access.\n      Reject an ambiguous\n      read/write collision and retry on hold. A ready external word wins.\n      Gate target use by the selected instruction's legal control encoding.\n      On a miss with imem stalled, present an invalid NOP. Flush validity\n      and the pending read on reset or FENCE.I.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise two hot branches 256 bytes apart and a straight-line run\n      longer than 16 words entered from two directions. Withhold imem after\n      warmup and require word 17 to advance immediately. Check the entire\n      RVFI instruction and PC sequence and store stream through interlocks.\n      Exercise a true 16 KiB index alias, an uninitialized slot in a live\n      group, a held lookup, same-index read/write, and replacement code\n      after FENCE.I. Update the collision stimulus to collide in the new\n      PC-indexed table and require recovery of the newly trained word.\nSat Nov 21 14:40:36 UTC 2026\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
