{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith('r3s2') or d['id'].endswith('r4s1'): print(d['id'], d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\ncat tools/eval/gowin.py | head -220\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r3s2 Give each BTB index two successor sequences, one per branch outcome. At a\nBTB hit, the predicted direction selects the sequence. Read its first word\non the same clock edge that installs the predicted next PC. As fetch advances,\npredict successive words along that path, up to 16 ordinary instructions.\nAn EX correction selects the sequence for the actual outcome. Each saved\nword carries its own PC tag. Checking it against the current fetch PC rejects\na stale sequence when a JALR changes target or two branches share an index.\nThe ready external response has first priority, then the BTB branch word,\nthen the predicted successor word.\n\nA scratch RTL prototype completed ten canonical CoreMark iterations in\n3,511,720 marker cycles with all five CRCs, 13.15% fewer cycles than the\nchampion. Sharing one 16-word sequence between the two directions cost\n39,471 extra cycles. Separate sequences reached 41.280, 42.159 and\n42.159 MHz across the three Gowin placement options. The median gives\n120.05 iterations/s, 11.84% above 107.34. At this cycle count the\nimplementation must exceed 39.429 MHz to clear the acceptance margin.\nPlacement option zero used 3,529 LUT4s and 14 BSRAMs. Its worst path ran\nfrom the register file through EX to the successor read-valid register.\nThe prototype passed 53 single-retirement formal checks and 92 cocotb\ncases, including four directed replay cases. Selftest and the three random\nprograms matched the reference retirement trace with both stall generators on.\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r4s1 Make MEM/WB the first bypass point for every M-extension result.\nMultiply the operands already captured in EX/MEM during MEM. For division,\nuse the existing six clocks in EX to consume the top 30 dividend bits,\nfive per clock. Carry the instruction across EX/MEM and use its MEM clock\nfor the last two quotient bits and sign correction. An independent\ninstruction can issue immediately after a multiply. An adjacent consumer\nof any M result uses the existing load-use bubble and MEM/WB bypass.\nThis schedule\n…[9309 of 12381 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d1c10bec6a1ce717]…\nench_sv\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    ap.add_argument(\"target\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--worktree\", default=\".\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--paths\", type=int, default=5)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--all-options\", action=\"store_true\",\n                    help=\"build every placement option the evaluator uses (default: option 0 only)\")\n    a = ap.parse_args(argv)\n    wt = Path(a.worktree).resolve()\n    opts = PLACE_OPTIONS if a.all_options else PLACE_OPTIONS[:1]\n    res = build_all(wt, wt / \"cores\" / a.target / \"rtl\", _bench_sv(str(wt), a.target),\n                                wt / \"cores\" / a.target / \"generated\" / \"timing\", options=opts)\n    for r in res:\n        if r[\"placement_failed\"]:\n            print(f\"place_option {r['place_option']}: FAILED ({r.get('reason')})\")\n            continue\n        print(f\"place_option {r['place_option']}: Fmax {r['fmax_mhz']} MHz, {r['levels']} logic levels; \"\n              f\"logic {r['logic']}, registers {r['regs']}, LUT-RAM {r['lutram']}, BSRAM {r['bsram']}, DSP {r['dsp']}\")\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
