{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))[-4:]:\n print(r['id'])\n for k in ['motivation','hypothesis']: print(r.get(k) if k=='motivation' else r.get(k).get('hypothesis'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r13s2\nThe champion already retires 2,922,016 instructions in 3,044,309\nCoreMark marker cycles. At one retirement per cycle, perfect issue\nwould gain only 4.19% at the current clock. In Gowin placement 1,\nwhich reproduces the 92.229 MHz median, the four longest paths\nstart at bit 0 of EX/MEM.alu_result, pass through the\nforwarded base, address alignment and ex_writer, and end at the next\ninstruction's saved operand. Every adjacent dependency pays for a\nfault check even when the producer is an ordinary ADD.\n\nPredict that a valid, decoded register writer will produce its rd.\nDrive bypass_writer from in.valid && in.ctrl.reg_write, ahead of\nlink_fault and data_fault. The existing nonzero-rd comparisons in\ncollection can then choose the adjacent bypass and mask older sources\nwhile EX computes the low bits of the forwarded address.\n\nVerify alignment in parallel using the existing architectural checks.\nOn an advancing instruction with link_fault or data_fault, force the\nexisting EX redirect to sequential_pc, even if fetch already predicted\nfall-through. This squashes the younger instruction that collected\nthe speculative operand. On refetch it sees the youngest surviving\nvalue of rd through normal register read and forwarding.\nA misaligned store takes the same recovery path. The faulting\ninstruction advances once with its original trap and suppressed side\neffects. Keep ctrl_with_trap.reg_write on the MEM and architectural\nwrite paths. The existing advancing gate prevents a held or\nload-repairing instruction from initiating recovery.\n\nA scratch prototype made those two EX control changes. It completed\nten iterations in 3,044,309 marker cycles with both stall generators,\nall five canonical CRCs and the validation banner. Gowin placements\n0, 1 and 2 reached 94.763, 97.260 and 97.260 MHz. The median gives\n319.48 iterations/s, 5.45% above the champion. The design used\n4,883 logic cells, 1,934 registers, 25 BSRAMs and two DSPs. Selftest\nand all three random programs matched the ISS with and without bus\nstalls.\n…[6264 of 9336 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0410364a83ae8907]…\nranch or\nthe target for a forward branch into the normal replay row. After\nthe observed exit, write the continuation for the next invocation.\nEncode an override as a weak counter in the chosen direction. On\nevery other fill use the existing counter. Fetch can follow the\nstored link at the existing latency.\n\nA prototype with a minimum run length of one completed ten iterations in 3,011,033 marker\ncycles with both stall generators, all five canonical CRCs and the\nvalidation banner. That saves 33,276 cycles over the champion.\nPutting the history in the wide fetch row saved only another 1,117\ncycles and reached 91.004 MHz on Gowin placement 1. Keep the history\non its own training port. Requiring at least two continuations also\navoids allocating loops on a brief alternation. The proposed placement\nof the entire counter calculation ahead of EX leaves the resolved\ncondition driving a one-bit fill register. With 3,011,033 cycles, promotion requires\na median above 100.622 MHz. Timing closure is the main uncertainty.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
